{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SkYhDM211aJrTPRv47kQhc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I now have the full paper and the review context. Let me dispatch two reviewers in parallel as required by the full review mode:\n\n- R1: Methods + Experiments\n- R2: Novelty + Related Work\n\nThen I'll do the 4-step roundtable process. Let me launch both reviewers as agents.\n\nLet me summarize what I've read:\n\n**Paper**: ContamCal - Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n**Core idea**: Two-stage framework:\n1. Stage 1: Multi-signal contamination detection (4 signals: S1 embedding retrieval, S2 Min-K++, S3 ConStat, S4 Self-Critique) aggregated via RRF\n\n\n2. Stage 2: Contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark γ_k susceptibility parameters, fitted with Stan NUTS\n\n**Key results**: The method achieves 2.1pp MAE recovering uncontaminated scores compared to 4.6pp for the best baseline, maintains 92% coverage of 95% confidence intervals, shows substantial heterogeneity across benchmarks in contamination susceptibility (CV=0.52), and reveals that certain signals like Min-K++ and Self-Critique only become effective at model scales of 1.5B parameters or larger, with Min-K++ even reversing its effectiveness at high contamination levels.\n\n**Validation**: Cross-family validation between Qwen and Llama models achieves r=0.91, and Fisher Information analysis confirms the identifiability thresholds for the model parameters.\n\n**Experimental coverage**: I've run the full pipeline including signal effectiveness across scales and dosages, RRF aggregation ablations, IRT calibration against multiple baselines, scale bridging experiments, signal survival analysis, RL observational studies at both 72B and smaller scales, closed-weight signal combinations, and cross-family validation.\n\nChecking against the planned experiments from the review context, all nine stages are accounted for in the paper. The coverage is solid, so now I'm ready to brief the reviewers.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2860,"cache_read_input_tokens":52978,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2860},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e2eb3c2-aea0-4799-9cc1-c104180c7ecf","uuid":"df1228ae-be96-424f-988f-69f1270c65dc"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SkYhDM211aJrTPRv47kQhc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the full paper (12 pages) and the experiment design context. Dispatching two independent reviewers now."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2860,"cache_read_input_tokens":52978,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2860},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e2eb3c2-aea0-4799-9cc1-c104180c7ecf","uuid":"10a1a469-ea47-4e6f-b812-434e868b6f38"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SkYhDM211aJrTPRv47kQhc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XBq1KVgdofAKMwiSTHMT2h","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Methods soundness and Experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: ContamCal: Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n**Core idea**: Two-stage framework for calibrating LLM benchmark scores under training data contamination:\n- Stage 1: Compute per-item contamination exposure scores via 4 detection signals (S1: embedding retrieval, S2: Min-K++, S3: ConStat, S4: Self-Critique) aggregated by Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)\n- Stage 2: Fit contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark γ_k susceptibility parameters using Stan NUTS. Counterfactual scores obtained by setting exposure=0.\n\n**Three core claims**:\n1. ContamCal recovers uncontaminated performance within 2.1pp MAE with 92% CI coverage, outperforming 7 baselines\n2. Systematic signal×scale×dosage characterization reveals scale-dependent signal effectiveness and Min-K++ directional reversal (Cohen's d up to -2.85)\n3. Fisher Information identifiability analysis establishes structural (AUC≥0.55) and practical (AUC≥0.70) thresholds, validated across model families (r=0.91)\n\n## Method Details\n\n**Stage 1 - Per-Item Exposure Mapping**:\n- S1 (Embedding Retrieval): Cosine similarity between test item and nearest training-set item in sentence-transformer space. Scale-invariant (AUC≥0.95).\n- S2 (Min-K%++): Token-level perplexity anomaly detection. Scale-dependent: AUC≈0.47 at 0.5B, ≥0.72 at ≥1.5B.\n- S3 (ConStat): Performance gap between original and paraphrased items. Available without logit access.\n- S4 (Self-Critique): Entropy collapse on familiar items. Scale-dependent with threshold at ~1.4B.\n- Aggregation: RRF(i) = Σ 1/(k + r_s(i)), k=60. Training-free to avoid meta-circularity.\n\n**Stage 2 - Contamination-Augmented 4PL IRT**:\n- P(Y_ij = 1) = c_i + (d_i - c_i) / (1 + exp(-a_i(θ_j - b_i + γ_k · e_ij)))\n- Per-benchmark γ_k (not global γ)\n- Measurement Error model: e_ij = τ_ij + ε_ij, ε ~ N(0, σ_e²)\n- Priors: a_i ~ LogNormal(0, 0.5), b_i ~ N(0,1), c_i ~ Beta(2,8), d_i ~ Beta(8,2), θ_j ~ N(0,1), γ_k ~ HalfNormal(0,1)\n- Fitted per-benchmark with Stan NUTS. N_models=32 (pooled across scales and seeds).\n- Counterfactual: θ_j^cal = (1/N_k) Σ P(Y_ij=1 | e_ij=0, estimated params)\n\n**Identifiability Analysis**: Fisher Information for γ_k depends on signal quality (e_ij variance) and sample size. Structural threshold AUC≥0.55, practical threshold AUC≥0.70, validated by 144-condition simulation.\n\n## Experimental Setup\n- Controlled contamination injection: Qwen2.5 (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B) via LoRA SFT at 4 dosage levels (d000, d005, d025, d100). 2-3 random seeds per condition. Multi-seed pooling → N_models ≥ 32.\n- Benchmarks: MMLU (1000 items subsampled), ARC-Challenge (1172), GSM8K (1319), HumanEval (164, appendix only)\n- 7 baselines: Raw, Remove-and-rescore, Single-signal 2PL+γ, Vanilla 4PL (γ=0), Linear regression, Emperor mitigation, DCR\n\n## Key Results\n\n**Signal effectiveness (Tables 1-3)**:\n- S1: AUC≥0.95 across all scales (scale-invariant)\n- S2/S4: Near random at 0.5B (AUC≈0.47), cross practical threshold at 1.5B (AUC≥0.70), consolidate at 3B (AUC≥0.77)\n- Min-K++ reversal at d100: Cohen's d = -1.98 (0.5B), -2.31 (3B), -2.85 (7B)\n- Instruct models: S2 degrades 15%, S4 preserved (AUC=0.71), S1 stable\n\n**RRF Aggregation (Tables 4-5)**:\n- 0.5B: RRF ≈ S1-only (+0.2pp), graceful degradation\n- 1.5B: +1.5pp over S1-only\n- 3B: +2.0pp over S1-only; RRF(4-sig) AUC=0.985 MMLU, 0.999 ARC\n- Weighted RRF only +0.3pp over vanilla RRF (meta-circularity argument)\n- FP robustness: 96% AUC retention at 10% FP, 91% at 20%\n\n**Main calibration (Table 6)**:\n- ContamCal: MAE=2.1±0.4pp, 95% Cov=92%, 80% Cov=82%, CI Width=5.8pp, Kendall τ=0.91, Mono. viol.=0\n- Best baseline DCR: MAE=4.6pp; improvement Δ=2.5pp (p<0.01)\n- MCMC: R̂_max=1.007, min ESS=520 (γ), 380 (θ), 0 divergent transitions\n- SBC diagnostics: p=0.23 (rank uniformity confirmed)\n\n**Per-benchmark γ_k (Table 7)**: MMLU=0.28±0.06, ARC=0.38±0.07, GSM8K=0.06±0.04 (CI includes 0), HumanEval=0.12±0.08. CV=0.52. 7/8 conditions CI excludes zero.\n\n**γ specification ablation (Table 9)**: Per-benchmark γ_k (2.1pp) > Hierarchical (2.3pp) > Global (3.1pp). Oracle γ gap = 0.7pp.\n\n**7B validation (Table 10)**: Largest contamination inflation (MMLU +4.4pp, ARC +3.4pp). S2 AUC=0.85, S4=0.82. Adding 7B: MAE 2.1→2.0pp, coverage 92→93%.\n\n**RL observational (Table 11)**: Instruct γ_MMLU=0.18, γ_ARC=0.25; DeepSeek-R1 γ=0.14. CI width 1.8× SFT. Specificity: γ_clean≈0.02, CI includes 0.\n\n**72B observational (Table 12)**: MMLU adj. -1.6pp, ARC -0.7pp, GSM8K -0.4pp. S2 AUC=0.88, S4=0.85.\n\n**2-signal mode (Table 13)**: MAE=3.4pp (gap +1.3pp vs 4-signal), 87% coverage, CI width +41%. Still beats DCR (4.6pp).\n\n**Cross-family Llama (Table 14)**: γ_k correlation r=0.91 (Qwen vs Llama). Llama MAE=2.4pp, coverage=90%.\n\n## Experiment Design Coverage Check\nThe review_context.yaml lists 9 imagined experiments. The paper covers all 9:\n1. stage1_signal_pipeline → §4.1 (Tables 1-2)\n2. rrf_aggregation_ablation → §4.3 (Tables 4-5)\n3. irt_calibration_and_baselines → §4.4-4.5 (Tables 6-9)\n4. scale_bridging_7b → §4.7 (Table 10)\n5. signal_survival_analysis → §4.1-4.2 (Tables 1-3)\n6. rl_observational_case_study → §4.8 (Table 11)\n7. observational_72b → §4.9 (Table 12)\n8. closed_weight_2signal_mode → §4.10 (Table 13)\n9. multi_family_validation → §4.11 (Table 14)\n\n## Your Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the 4PL+γ IRT formulation well-motivated and correctly specified? Are the priors reasonable? Is the measurement error model appropriate? Is the RRF aggregation justified?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are controls adequate? Is the controlled injection setup valid? Are statistical tests appropriate? Are there confounds?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Are enough details provided to reproduce?\n4. **Baselines**: Are comparisons fair? Are important baselines missing?\n5. **Claims vs evidence**: Do the results support the three core claims?\n6. **Weaknesses**: Identify concrete methodological or experimental issues.\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted, with specific evidence)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with specific evidence and severity: minor/moderate/major)\n- Questions for authors\n- Missing experiments or analyses\n- Preliminary score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a blind review — assess the work on its merits."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2860,"cache_read_input_tokens":52978,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2860},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e2eb3c2-aea0-4799-9cc1-c104180c7ecf","uuid":"231d2a97-84ca-4049-822b-0f2c25109a14"}
