{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DECVjFKrsLzRJXpsHFyHjo","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"cross_benchmark_summary\": {\n3\t    \"S1_Embedding\": {\n4\t      \"d000\": {\"mmlu\": 0.5049, \"gsm8k\": 0.5502, \"arc\": 0.4270},\n5\t      \"d005\": {\"mmlu\": 0.9530, \"gsm8k\": 0.9882, \"arc\": 0.9950},\n6\t      \"d100\": {\"mmlu\": 0.4943, \"gsm8k\": 0.4127, \"arc\": 0.5010}\n7\t    },\n8\t    \"S2_MinKpp\": {\n9\t      \"d000\": {\"mmlu\": 0.5090, \"gsm8k\": 0.5697, \"arc\": 0.5075},\n10\t      \"d005\": {\"mmlu\": 0.4655, \"gsm8k\": 0.4649, \"arc\": 0.4659},\n11\t      \"d100\": {\"mmlu\": 0.5053, \"gsm8k\": 0.4841, \"arc\": 0.4950}\n12\t    },\n13\t    \"S4_SelfCritique\": {\n14\t      \"d000\": {\"mmlu\": 0.4957, \"gsm8k\": 0.4800, \"arc\": 0.5175},\n15\t      \"d005\": {\"mmlu\": 0.5165, \"gsm8k\": 0.4947, \"arc\": 0.4325},\n16\t      \"d100\": {\"mmlu\": 0.5125, \"gsm8k\": 0.4393, \"arc\": 0.5515}\n17\t    }\n18\t  },\n19\t  \"surviving_signals\": 0,\n20\t  \"signals_total\": 3,\n21\t  \"interpretation\": \"No individual signal maintains AUC>0.6 across all dosage regimes. S1 strong at d005 only (low contamination = detectable outliers). Validates need for IRT multi-signal fusion (C2).\"\n22\t}\n23\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XBq1KVgdofAKMwiSTHMT2h","session_id":"9e2eb3c2-aea0-4799-9cc1-c104180c7ecf","uuid":"1656979a-8f25-42d8-8bf8-12fb3c1e6c53","timestamp":"2026-05-14T19:52:42.202Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SkYhDM211aJrTPRv47kQhc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DQhgCw7Y5dRQfV2Ceqdzih","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Novelty, significance, related work positioning, and presentation quality**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: ContamCal: Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n**Core idea**: Two-stage framework for calibrating LLM benchmark scores under training data contamination:\n- Stage 1: Compute per-item contamination exposure scores via 4 detection signals (S1: embedding retrieval, S2: Min-K++, S3: ConStat, S4: Self-Critique) aggregated by Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)\n- Stage 2: Fit contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark γ_k susceptibility parameters using Stan NUTS. Counterfactual scores obtained by setting exposure=0.\n\n**Three core claims**:\n1. ContamCal recovers uncontaminated performance within 2.1pp MAE with 92% CI coverage, outperforming 7 baselines\n2. Systematic signal×scale×dosage characterization reveals scale-dependent signal effectiveness and Min-K++ directional reversal (Cohen's d up to -2.85)\n3. Fisher Information identifiability analysis establishes structural (AUC≥0.55) and practical (AUC≥0.70) thresholds, validated across model families (r=0.91)\n\n## Related Work as Presented in Paper (Section 2)\n\nThe paper positions itself at the intersection of 5 areas:\n1. **Contamination Detection**: Min-K++ (Shi et al., 2024), ConStat (Dekoninck et al., 2024a), Self-Critique (Weller et al., 2024), KDS (Dong et al., 2024). Paper claims these detect but don't calibrate.\n2. **Contamination Mitigation**: Oren et al. (2024) tested 20 strategies, found none effective. Dynamic benchmarks (LiveBench) bypass but expensive.\n3. **IRT for LLM Evaluation**: Lalor et al. (2024), Polo et al. (2024) — standard IRT without contamination. HiBayES provides Bayesian UQ but ignores contamination. Burnell et al. (2023) multidimensional IRT.\n4. **Post-hoc Calibration**: DCR (Yang et al., 2025) provides adjusted accuracy via fuzzy inference but lacks probabilistic UQ. Conformal prediction assumes exchangeability violated by contamination.\n5. **Data Attribution**: TracIn, TRAK — mentioned as potential future signals.\n\nThe paper connects to educational measurement literature: item compromise (Eckerly, 2017; Sinharay, 2017), explanatory IRT (De Boeck and Wilson, 2004), DIF analysis (Holland and Wainer, 1993; Thissen et al., 1993; Zumbo, 1999).\n\n## Method Innovation Points\n- γ_k parameter in 4PL IRT as contamination susceptibility (per-benchmark, not per-item or global)\n- RRF as training-free signal aggregation avoiding meta-circularity\n- Measurement error model propagating exposure score uncertainty\n- Fisher Information identifiability analysis for signal quality thresholds\n- Counterfactual calibration: set e_ij=0 to recover uncontaminated scores\n\n## Key Empirical Contributions\n- First systematic signal×scale×dosage characterization\n- Min-K++ directional reversal phenomenon (actively misleading at high contamination)\n- Per-benchmark γ_k heterogeneity (CV=0.52) establishing contamination susceptibility as benchmark property\n- Cross-family γ_k correlation (r=0.91, Qwen vs Llama)\n- Scale-dependent signal emergence thresholds (~1.2B for S2, ~1.4B for S4)\n\n## Paper Structure (12 pages)\n1. Introduction (pp 1-2): Problem, contributions\n2. Related Work (p 2): 5 areas covered\n3. Method (pp 2-3): Problem formulation, Stage 1 (RRF), Stage 2 (4PL+γ IRT), ME model, priors, identifiability\n4. Experimental Setup (p 4): Controlled injection, benchmarks, baselines, metrics\n5. Results (pp 4-9): Signal effectiveness, Min-K++ reversal, signal survival, RRF, main calibration, per-benchmark γ_k, γ ablation, 7B, RL observational, 72B, 2-signal mode, cross-family Llama\n6. Discussion (p 9): Contamination as benchmark property, scale-dependent signal selection, honest uncertainty\n7. Limitations (p 9): SFT proxy, 92% vs 95% coverage gap, unidimensional θ, limited architectures\n8. Conclusion (p 10)\n9. References (pp 10-12): ~30 references\n\n## Tables in Paper\n- Table 1: Signal AUC heatmap (signal×scale×benchmark at d005)\n- Table 2: Cross-model Cohen's d at d100\n- Table 3: Signal survival per scale + Instruct comparison\n- Table 4: RRF ablation (3B, d005)\n- Table 5: FP robustness\n- Table 6: Main results — 8 methods × 6 metrics\n- Table 7: Per-benchmark γ_k posteriors\n- Table 8: Per-benchmark MAE decomposition\n- Table 9: γ specification ablation (global/hierarchical/per-benchmark/oracle)\n- Table 10: 7B dose-response\n- Table 11: RL model calibration\n- Table 12: 72B observational\n- Table 13: 2-signal vs 4-signal\n- Table 14: Cross-family γ_k (Qwen vs Llama)\n\n## Experiment coverage\nAll 9 planned experiments from the design doc are present in the paper. No significant gaps.\n\n## Your Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Novelty**: How novel is the contamination-augmented IRT formulation? Is the connection to educational measurement item compromise literature genuinely new in the LLM context? Is the combination of existing signals (S1-S4) + RRF + IRT more than the sum of its parts?\n2. **Significance and impact**: Will this matter to the community? Does it change how people think about contamination?\n3. **Related work completeness**: Are important related works missing? Is the positioning fair?\n4. **Presentation quality**: Is the paper well-written? Is the structure logical? Are the 14 tables excessive? Are figures missing (the paper seems table-heavy with no figures)?\n5. **Framing and claims**: Are claims appropriately scoped? Is the paper overselling or underselling?\n6. **Broader context**: Does the SFT injection setup with rephrased items adequately represent real-world contamination?\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted, with specific evidence)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with specific evidence and severity: minor/moderate/major)\n- Questions for authors\n- Missing related work or context\n- Preliminary score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a blind review — assess the work on its merits."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2860,"cache_read_input_tokens":52978,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2860},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e2eb3c2-aea0-4799-9cc1-c104180c7ecf","uuid":"53684c88-55f3-4e64-abb0-e8c18666374e"}
