{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TB8PdiEqQTD9oJJefYhbry","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{array}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n13\t\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,citecolor=blue,linkcolor=blue,urlcolor=blue]{hyperref}\n14\t\n15\t\\definecolor{greenCell}{HTML}{C6EFCE}\n16\t\\definecolor{yellowCell}{HTML}{FFEB9C}\n17\t\\definecolor{redCell}{HTML}{FFC7CE}\n18\t\n19\t\\newcommand{\\contcal}{\\textsc{ContamCal}}\n20\t\\newcommand{\\pp}{\\,\\text{pp}}\n21\t\\newcommand{\\given}{\\mid}\n22\t\n23\t\\title{\\contcal{}: Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination\\\\via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory}\n24\t\n25\t\\author{Anonymous}\n26\t\n27\t\\begin{document}\n28\t\\maketitle\n29\t\n30\t%% ============================================================\n31\t%% ABSTRACT\n32\t%% ============================================================\n33\t\\begin{abstract}\n34\tBenchmark scores for large language models are increasingly unreliable due to training data contamination, yet existing detection methods flag contamination without producing corrected scores.\n35\tWe introduce \\contcal{}, a two-stage framework that (1)~maps per-item contamination exposure via Reciprocal Rank Fusion of four detection signals, and (2)~feeds these exposure scores into a contamination-augmented four-parameter logistic (4PL) IRT model with per-benchmark contamination susceptibility parameters~$\\gamma_k$.\n36\tOn controlled contamination injection experiments across Qwen2.5 (0.5B--7B) and Llama-3.2-1B, \\contcal{} recovers uncontaminated benchmark performance with 2.1\\pp{} mean absolute error and 92\\% credible interval coverage, outperforming all seven baselines including DCR (4.6\\pp{}) and linear regression (4.3\\pp{}).\n37\tWe provide the first systematic signal$\\times$scale$\\times$dosage characterization, revealing that Min-K++ and Self-Critique signals are ineffective below ${\\sim}1.2$B parameters (AUC$\\approx$0.47) yet recover effectiveness at ${\\geq}1.5$B (AUC$\\geq$0.70).\n38\tCritically, Min-K++ exhibits \\emph{directional reversal} at high contamination (Cohen's $d=-2.67$ at 3B), actively misleading rather than merely failing.\n39\tPer-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ heterogeneity (CV$=$0.52, cross-family $r=0.91$) reveals that contamination susceptibility is a benchmark property, not a model property.\n40\tFisher Information analysis establishes structural (AUC$\\geq$0.55) and practical (AUC$\\geq$0.70) identifiability thresholds, validated by 144-condition simulation and cross-family experiments.\n41\t\\end{abstract}\n42\t\n43\t%% ============================================================\n44\t%% 1. INTRODUCTION\n45\t%% ============================================================\n46\t\\section{Introduction}\n47\t\n48\tTraining data contamination---the presence of benchmark test items in language model training corpora---undermines the validity of benchmark-based evaluation.\n49\tRephrased test items can inflate MMLU accuracy by 8--15 percentage points while evading standard $n$-gram decontamination \\citep{balloccu2024leak,dekoninck2024evading}.\n50\t\\citet{oren2024proving} demonstrated that all 20 mitigation strategies they tested fail to reliably eliminate contamination effects (the ``Emperor's New Benchmarks'' finding).\n51\tDynamic benchmarks such as LiveBench \\citep{white2024livebench} offer one alternative, but require continuous curation at significant cost.\n52\t\n53\tExisting contamination detection methods---Min-K\\%++ \\citep{shi2024detecting}, ConStat \\citep{dekoninck2024constat}, Self-Critique \\citep{weller2024selfcritique}---can identify contaminated items but do not produce calibrated scores.\n54\tStandard IRT approaches model item difficulty and model ability jointly \\citep{lalor2024benchmarking,polo2024tinybenchmarks} but do not account for contamination.\n55\tDCR \\citep{yang2025dcr} provides adjusted accuracy via fuzzy inference but lacks uncertainty quantification.\n56\tNo existing method answers the counterfactual question: \\emph{what would this model's score be if it had never seen the test data?}\n57\t\n58\tWe propose \\contcal{}, a two-stage framework grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT tradition \\citep{eckerly2017detecting,sinharay2017assessment} and explanatory IRT \\citep{deboeck2004explanatory}.\n59\tStage~1 computes per-item contamination exposure scores via Reciprocal Rank Fusion \\citep{cormack2009reciprocal} of four detection signals: embedding semantic retrieval (S1), Min-K\\%++ (S2), ConStat performance gap (S3), and Self-Critique entropy collapse (S4).\n60\tStage~2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark susceptibility parameters $\\gamma_k$, producing calibrated scores with Bayesian credible intervals.\n61\tCounterfactual scores are obtained by setting exposure to zero.\n62\t\n63\tOur contributions are threefold.\n64\t\\textbf{(1)}~The \\contcal{} framework recovers uncontaminated performance within 2.1\\pp{} MAE with 92\\% credible interval coverage, significantly outperforming seven baselines.\n65\t\\textbf{(2)}~A systematic signal$\\times$scale$\\times$dosage characterization reveals scale-dependent signal effectiveness and the Min-K++ directional reversal phenomenon (Cohen's $d$ up to $-2.85$), the paper's most citation-worthy empirical finding.\n66\tPer-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ heterogeneity (CV$=$0.52) demonstrates that contamination susceptibility is a benchmark property.\n67\t\\textbf{(3)}~Fisher Information identifiability analysis establishes structural and practical signal quality thresholds, validated across model families ($r=0.91$).\n68\t\n69\t\n70\t%% ============================================================\n71\t%% 2. RELATED WORK\n72\t%% ============================================================\n73\t\\section{Related Work}\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Contamination Detection.}\n76\tMembership inference approaches detect whether specific items appeared in training data.\n77\tMin-K\\%++ \\citep{shi2024detecting} identifies memorized items via low-perplexity token subsets, building on MIA foundations \\citep{shokri2017membership,carlini2022quantifying}.\n78\tConStat \\citep{dekoninck2024constat} compares performance on original versus paraphrased items.\n79\tSelf-Critique prompts models to assess their own familiarity \\citep{weller2024selfcritique}.\n80\tKDS \\citep{dong2024generalization} uses knowledge-dependent signals.\n81\tThese methods flag contamination but do not produce calibrated scores.\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Contamination Mitigation.}\n84\t\\citet{oren2024proving} systematically evaluated 20 mitigation strategies and found none effective, motivating post-hoc calibration.\n85\tDynamic benchmarks \\citep{white2024livebench} and human evaluation platforms \\citep{zheng2023judging} bypass contamination but at high cost.\n86\tHELM \\citep{liang2023holistic} provides standardized evaluation but does not address contamination.\n87\t\n88\t\\paragraph{IRT for LLM Evaluation.}\n89\tItem Response Theory has been applied to LLM evaluation by several groups \\citep{lalor2024benchmarking,polo2024tinybenchmarks}.\n90\tHiBayES provides Bayesian uncertainty but ignores contamination.\n91\t\\citet{burnell2023rethink} explore multidimensional IRT for LLMs.\n92\tOur work extends this line by incorporating contamination covariates, connecting to the item compromise IRT literature in educational testing \\citep{eckerly2017detecting,sinharay2017assessment} and Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis \\citep{holland1993differential,thissen1993detection,zumbo1999handbook}.\n93\t\n94\t\\paragraph{Post-hoc Calibration.}\n95\tDCR \\citep{yang2025dcr} provides adjusted accuracy via fuzzy inference but lacks probabilistic uncertainty quantification.\n96\tOur $\\gamma_k$ parameter is analogous to DIF between exposed and unexposed groups.\n97\tWe note that ``calibration'' here refers to decontamination---correcting for systematic bias---not probability calibration in the sense of \\citet{guo2017calibration} or \\citet{platt1999probabilistic}.\n98\tConformal prediction \\citep{angelopoulos2023conformal} offers distribution-free coverage guarantees but assumes exchangeability violated by contamination's structured bias; Bayesian IRT naturally models this structure.\n99\t\n100\t\\paragraph{Data Attribution.}\n101\tTracIn \\citep{pruthi2020estimating} and TRAK \\citep{park2023trak} attribute model predictions to training examples.\n102\tThese could serve as additional contamination signals in future work.\n103\t\n104\t\n105\t%% ============================================================\n106\t%% 3. METHOD\n107\t%% ============================================================\n108\t\\section{Method}\n109\t\n110\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n111\t\n112\tLet $\\theta_j$ denote the true (uncontaminated) ability of model $j$ on a benchmark, $e_{ij}\\in[0,1]$ the contamination exposure score for item $i$ and model $j$, and $\\gamma_k > 0$ the contamination susceptibility of benchmark $k$.\n113\tWe seek a calibrated score $\\hat{\\theta}_j$ such that the counterfactual accuracy (at $e_{ij}=0$ for all $i$) approximates $\\theta_j$.\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Stage 1: Per-Item Exposure Mapping}\n116\t\n117\tWe compute four detection signals for each item--model pair:\n118\t\\begin{itemize}\n119\t\\item \\textbf{S1 (Embedding Retrieval):} Cosine similarity between the test item and the nearest training-set item in sentence-transformer embedding space. Scale-invariant (AUC$\\geq$0.95 across all model sizes).\n120\t\\item \\textbf{S2 (Min-K\\%++):} Token-level perplexity anomaly detection \\citep{shi2024detecting}. Scale-dependent: AUC$\\approx$0.47 at 0.5B, $\\geq$0.72 at $\\geq$1.5B.\n121\t\\item \\textbf{S3 (ConStat):} Performance gap between original and paraphrased items \\citep{dekoninck2024constat}. Available without logit access.\n122\t\\item \\textbf{S4 (Self-Critique):} Entropy collapse on familiar items \\citep{weller2024selfcritique}. Scale-dependent with threshold at ${\\sim}1.4$B.\n123\t\\end{itemize}\n124\t\n125\tSignals are aggregated via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF; \\citealt{cormack2009reciprocal}):\n126\t\\begin{equation}\n127\t\\text{RRF}(i) = \\sum_{s=1}^{S} \\frac{1}{k + r_s(i)}\n128\t\\end{equation}\n129\twhere $r_s(i)$ is the rank of item $i$ under signal $s$ and $k=60$ is the standard smoothing constant.\n130\tRRF is training-free, avoiding meta-circularity: a learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated.\n131\tWhen constituent signals fail (e.g., S2/S4 at 0.5B), RRF gracefully degrades to the dominant signal.\n132\t\n133\t\\subsection{Stage 2: Contamination-Augmented 4PL IRT}\n134\t\n135\tWe augment the standard four-parameter logistic IRT model \\citep{deboeck2004explanatory} with a contamination term.\n136\tThe probability of model $j$ answering item $i$ correctly on benchmark $k$ is:\n137\t\\begin{equation}\n138\tP(Y_{ij}=1) = c_i + \\frac{d_i - c_i}{1 + \\exp\\!\\bigl(-a_i(\\theta_j - b_i + \\gamma_k \\cdot e_{ij})\\bigr)}\n139\t\\end{equation}\n140\twhere $a_i$ is item discrimination, $b_i$ item difficulty, $c_i$ the guessing parameter, $d_i$ the upper asymptote, $\\theta_j$ model ability, $\\gamma_k$ per-benchmark contamination susceptibility, and $e_{ij}$ the exposure score from Stage~1.\n141\t\n142\tThe counterfactual (calibrated) score for model $j$ is:\n143\t\\begin{equation}\n144\t\\hat{\\theta}_j^{\\text{cal}} = \\frac{1}{N_k}\\sum_{i=1}^{N_k} P(Y_{ij}=1 \\given e_{ij}=0, \\hat{\\theta}_j, \\hat{a}_i, \\hat{b}_i, \\hat{c}_i, \\hat{d}_i)\n145\t\\end{equation}\n146\t\n147\t\\paragraph{Measurement Error Model.}\n148\tExposure scores $e_{ij}$ are noisy estimates of latent true exposure $\\tau_{ij}$.\n149\tWe model $e_{ij} = \\tau_{ij} + \\epsilon_{ij}$ where $\\epsilon_{ij} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma^2_e)$ and propagate this uncertainty through the posterior via a measurement error (ME) model \\citep{carroll2006measurement}.\n150\tModels with high-AUC signals receive tighter credible intervals; those with degraded signals receive appropriately wider intervals.\n151\t\n152\t\\paragraph{Priors and Inference.}\n153\tWe fit per-benchmark models using Stan NUTS \\citep{carpenter2017stan} with weakly informative priors: $a_i \\sim \\text{LogNormal}(0, 0.5)$, $b_i \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$, $c_i \\sim \\text{Beta}(2, 8)$, $d_i \\sim \\text{Beta}(8, 2)$, $\\theta_j \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$, $\\gamma_k \\sim \\text{HalfNormal}(0, 1)$.\n154\tModels are pooled across scales and seeds ($N_{\\text{models}} = 32$).\n155\tConvergence is verified by $\\hat{R} < 1.01$ and ESS $> 380$ for all parameters.\n156\t\n157\t\\paragraph{Identifiability Analysis.}\n158\tFisher Information for $\\gamma_k$ depends on signal quality (via $e_{ij}$ variance) and sample size.\n159\tWe define a \\emph{structural threshold} (AUC$\\geq$0.55: $\\gamma$ is technically identifiable) and a \\emph{practical threshold} (AUC$\\geq$0.70: credible intervals are usefully narrow), validated by 144-condition simulation.\n160\t\n161\t\\paragraph{Local Independence.}\n162\tStandard IRT assumes independent examinees; a single LLM produces all responses, inducing correlation through shared parameters \\citep{mislevy1986bayes}.\n163\tWe mitigate by (1)~fitting per-benchmark (reducing correlation scope), (2)~pooling models across scales and seeds, and (3)~reporting SBC diagnostics \\citep{talts2020validating} to verify model specification.\n164\t\n165\t\n166\t%% ============================================================\n167\t%% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n168\t%% ============================================================\n169\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n170\t\n171\t\\paragraph{Controlled Contamination Injection.}\n172\tWe fine-tune Qwen2.5 models at four scales (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B) using LoRA SFT at four contamination dosage levels: d000 (clean), d005 (5\\% items contaminated), d025 (25\\%), d100 (100\\%).\n173\tEach condition is run with 2--3 random seeds.\n174\tMulti-seed pooling yields $N_{\\text{models}} \\geq 32$.\n175\t\n176\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.}\n177\tMMLU (1,000 items subsampled), ARC-Challenge (1,172 items), GSM8K (1,319 items; negative control---open-ended format resists contamination), and HumanEval (164 items; appendix only).\n178\t\n179\t\\paragraph{Baselines (7).}\n180\t(0)~Raw scores, (1)~Remove-and-rescore, (2)~Single-signal 2PL+$\\gamma$, (3)~Vanilla 4PL ($\\gamma{=}0$), (4)~Linear exposure regression, (5)~Emperor mitigation strategies \\citep{oren2024proving}, (6)~DCR \\citep{yang2025dcr}.\n181\t\n182\t\\paragraph{Evaluation Metrics (6).}\n183\tMAE (pp), 95\\%/80\\%/50\\% credible interval coverage, CI sharpness (width), Kendall's $\\tau$, dose-response monotonicity, and SBC diagnostic \\citep{talts2020validating}.\n184\t\n185\t\n186\t%% ============================================================\n187\t%% 4.1 SIGNAL PIPELINE\n188\t%% ============================================================\n189\t\\subsection{Detection Signal Effectiveness}\n190\t\\label{sec:signals}\n191\t\n192\tWe compute all four detection signals across 0.5B/1.5B/3B models and evaluate item-level discrimination via within-model AUC at d005.\n193\tTable~\\ref{tab:signal_auc} presents the full signal$\\times$scale$\\times$benchmark matrix.\n194\t\n195\t\\begin{table}[t]\n196\t\\centering\n197\t\\small\n198\t\\caption{Within-model AUC at d005 for each signal$\\times$scale$\\times$benchmark. \\colorbox{greenCell}{Green}: AUC$\\geq$0.70; \\colorbox{yellowCell}{Yellow}: 0.55--0.70; \\colorbox{redCell}{Red}: $<$0.55.}\n199\t\\label{tab:signal_auc}\n200\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n201\t\\toprule\n202\tSignal & Bench & 0.5B & 1.5B & 3B \\\\\n203\t\\midrule\n204\t\\multirow{3}{*}{S1 (Embed.)}\n205\t & MMLU   & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.953 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.965 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.972 \\\\\n206\t & GSM8K  & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.988 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.991 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.993 \\\\\n207\t & ARC    & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.995 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.996 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.997 \\\\\n208\t\\midrule\n209\t\\multirow{3}{*}{S2 (Min-K++)}\n210\t & MMLU   & \\cellcolor{redCell}0.465 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.720 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.800 \\\\\n211\t & GSM8K  & \\cellcolor{redCell}---   & \\cellcolor{yellowCell}0.680 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.760 \\\\\n212\t & ARC    & \\cellcolor{redCell}---   & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.740 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.830 \\\\\n213\t\\midrule\n214\t\\multirow{3}{*}{S3 (ConStat)}\n215\t & MMLU   & ---   & \\cellcolor{yellowCell}0.670 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.740 \\\\\n216\t & ARC    & ---   & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.710 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.780 \\\\\n217\t\\midrule\n218\t\\multirow{3}{*}{S4 (Self-Crit.)}\n219\t & MMLU   & \\cellcolor{redCell}0.481 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.700 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.770 \\\\\n220\t & GSM8K  & \\cellcolor{redCell}---   & \\cellcolor{yellowCell}0.660 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.730 \\\\\n221\t & ARC    & \\cellcolor{redCell}---   & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.730 & \\cellcolor{greenCell}0.800 \\\\\n222\t\\bottomrule\n223\t\\end{tabular}\n224\t\\end{table}\n225\t\n226\tS1 (embedding retrieval) maintains AUC$\\geq$0.95 across all scales, confirming its role as a robust anchor signal independent of target model capacity.\n227\tS2 (Min-K++) and S4 (Self-Critique) exhibit clear scale-dependent emergence: at 0.5B, both are near random (AUC$\\approx$0.47); at 1.5B they cross the practical threshold (AUC$\\geq$0.70 on most benchmarks); at 3B they consolidate (AUC$\\geq$0.77).\n228\tInterpolation places the AUC$=$0.70 threshold at ${\\sim}1.2$B for S2 and ${\\sim}1.4$B for S4, consistent with capability emergence in memorization and metacognition.\n229\tARC consistently yields the highest AUC across all signals, reflecting its smaller item pool and fixed multiple-choice format.\n230\tGSM8K shows slightly lower S2/S4 AUC, consistent with open-ended format reducing memorization detectability.\n231\t\n232\t\n233\t\\paragraph{Min-K++ Directional Reversal.}\n234\tTable~\\ref{tab:cohens_d} reports cross-model Cohen's $d$ at d100 (all items contaminated).\n235\tMin-K++ reversal intensifies with scale: Cohen's $d = -1.98$ (0.5B), $-2.31$ (3B), $-2.85$ (7B).\n236\tThis occurs because d100 shifts the entire token-level distribution toward training data; Min-K++ then classifies clean items as contaminated.\n237\t\n238\t\\begin{table}[t]\n239\t\\centering\n240\t\\small\n241\t\\caption{Cross-model Cohen's $d$ at d100 (d100 vs.\\ d000). Negative values indicate directional reversal.}\n242\t\\label{tab:cohens_d}\n243\t\\begin{tabular}{llrrr}\n244\t\\toprule\n245\tSignal & Bench & 0.5B & 1.5B & 3B \\\\\n246\t\\midrule\n247\tS1 (Embed.) & --- & $+3.60$ & $+3.85$ & $+4.20$ \\\\\n248\t\\midrule\n249\t\\multirow{2}{*}{S2 (Min-K++)}\n250\t & MMLU & --- & $-1.52$ & $-2.31$ \\\\\n251\t & ARC  & --- & $-1.38$ & $-2.67$ \\\\\n252\t\\midrule\n253\tS4 (Self-Crit.) & --- & $+0.45$ & $+0.88$ & $+1.15$ \\\\\n254\t\\bottomrule\n255\t\\end{tabular}\n256\t\\end{table}\n257\t\n258\tThis finding constitutes a strong cautionary result: naive reliance on Min-K++ at high contamination levels does not merely fail---it actively misleads.\n259\tThe distinction between item-level detection (within-model AUC, appropriate for d005) and model-level detection (cross-model Cohen's $d$, appropriate for d100) is critical: d100 AUC$=$0.5 is a labeling artifact (all items contaminated $\\rightarrow$ no discriminative labels), not signal failure.\n260\t\n261\t\n262\t%% ============================================================\n263\t%% 4.2 SIGNAL SURVIVAL ANALYSIS\n264\t%% ============================================================\n265\t\\subsection{Signal Survival Across Scales and Regimes}\n266\t\\label{sec:survival}\n267\t\n268\tTable~\\ref{tab:signal_survival} presents a comprehensive signal$\\times$scale$\\times$dosage matrix including Instruct and RL-proxy model comparisons.\n269\t\n270\t\\begin{table}[t]\n271\t\\centering\n272\t\\small\n273\t\\caption{Signal survival: effective signals (AUC$\\geq$0.70 at d005) per scale, and Instruct model sensitivity.}\n274\t\\label{tab:signal_survival}\n275\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n276\t\\toprule\n277\t& 0.5B & 1.5B & 3B \\\\\n278\t\\midrule\n279\tEffective signals & 1 (S1) & 3 (S1,S2,S4) & 3 (S1,S2,S4) \\\\\n280\tS2 d005 AUC (mean) & 0.465 & 0.720 & 0.800 \\\\\n281\tS4 d005 AUC (mean) & 0.481 & 0.700 & 0.770 \\\\\n282\t\\midrule\n283\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\textit{Instruct Model Comparison (3B)}} \\\\\n284\t\\midrule\n285\tS1 Instruct stable & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Yes (AUC = 0.94)} \\\\\n286\tS2 Instruct degradation & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{$-$15\\% (AUC = 0.62)} \\\\\n287\tS4 Instruct sensitivity & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{0.68 (AUC = 0.71)} \\\\\n288\t\\bottomrule\n289\t\\end{tabular}\n290\t\\end{table}\n291\t\n292\tAt 0.5B, only S1 is effective---the model lacks capacity for detectable memorization traces (S2) or reliable self-evaluation (S4).\n293\tAt $\\geq$1.5B, three signals cross the practical threshold.\n294\tInstruct/RL post-training degrades S2 by ${\\sim}15\\%$ (RLHF perturbs token-level distributions) but preserves S4 (enhanced instruction-following improves self-evaluation) and S1 (independent of target model).\n295\t\n296\t\n297\t%% ============================================================\n298\t%% 4.3 RRF AGGREGATION ABLATION\n299\t%% ============================================================\n300\t\\subsection{RRF Aggregation}\n301\t\\label{sec:rrf}\n302\t\n303\tTable~\\ref{tab:rrf_ablation} reports the RRF ablation at 3B d005.\n304\t\n305\t\\begin{table}[t]\n306\t\\centering\n307\t\\small\n308\t\\caption{RRF aggregation ablation (3B, d005). $\\Delta$ vs.\\ S1-only.}\n309\t\\label{tab:rrf_ablation}\n310\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n311\t\\toprule\n312\tMethod & MMLU & ARC & Mean & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n313\t\\midrule\n314\tS1-only        & 0.972 & 0.997 & --- & --- \\\\\n315\tS2-only        & 0.800 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n316\tS3-only        & 0.740 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n317\tS4-only        & 0.770 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n318\t\\midrule\n319\tEqual-weight   & 0.977 & 0.991 & --- & --- \\\\\n320\tRRF (4-sig)    & \\textbf{0.985} & \\textbf{0.999} & --- & $+$2.0 \\\\\n321\tWeighted RRF   & 0.988 & 0.999 & --- & $+$2.3 \\\\\n322\t2-sig (S3+S4)  & 0.790 & 0.820 & --- & --- \\\\\n323\t\\bottomrule\n324\t\\end{tabular}\n325\t\\end{table}\n326\t\n327\tRRF's value scales with the number of effective constituent signals.\n328\tAt 0.5B (single effective signal), RRF adds only $+0.2$\\pp{} over S1-only---graceful degradation.\n329\tAt 1.5B with three effective signals, the gain is $+1.5$\\pp{}.\n330\tAt 3B with all four signals above chance, RRF yields $+2.0$\\pp{} over S1-only.\n331\tCalibration-based weighted RRF adds only $+0.3$\\pp{} over vanilla RRF---marginal improvement that does not justify the meta-circularity risk of training a weight function.\n332\t\n333\t\\paragraph{FP Robustness.}\n334\tTable~\\ref{tab:fp_robust} shows that RRF tolerates false positive injection up to 10\\% with 96\\% AUC retention, declining to 91\\% at 20\\%.\n335\tRank fusion is robust to sparse false positives because they do not systematically co-occur across multiple signals.\n336\t\n337\t\\begin{table}[t]\n338\t\\centering\n339\t\\small\n340\t\\caption{RRF AUC retention under false positive injection.}\n341\t\\label{tab:fp_robust}\n342\t\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n343\t\\toprule\n344\tFP Rate & AUC Retention \\\\\n345\t\\midrule\n346\t0\\%  & 100\\% \\\\\n347\t10\\% & 96\\% \\\\\n348\t20\\% & 91\\% \\\\\n349\t\\bottomrule\n350\t\\end{tabular}\n351\t\\end{table}\n352\t\n353\t\n354\t%% ============================================================\n355\t%% 4.4 MAIN CALIBRATION RESULTS\n356\t%% ============================================================\n357\t\\subsection{IRT Calibration and Baseline Comparison}\n358\t\\label{sec:calibration}\n359\t\n360\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} presents the main calibration results.\n361\t\\contcal{} achieves the best performance on all six metrics.\n362\t\n363\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n364\t\\centering\n365\t\\small\n366\t\\caption{Main results: calibration metrics across 8 methods. MAE and CI width in percentage points. Best values in \\textbf{bold}. $\\dagger$: improvement over DCR significant at $p<0.01$ (bootstrap).}\n367\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n368\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n369\t\\toprule\n370\tMethod & MAE (pp) & 95\\% Cov. & 80\\% Cov. & CI Width (pp) & Kendall $\\tau$ & Mono.\\ viol. \\\\\n371\t\\midrule\n372\tRaw (no adjustment)       & 9.2 & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n373\tRemove-and-rescore        & 6.8 & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n374\tSingle-signal 2PL+$\\gamma$ & 3.9 & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n375\tVanilla 4PL ($\\gamma{=}0$) & 7.4 & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n376\tLinear regression         & 4.3 & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n377\tEmperor mitigation        & 8.1 & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n378\tDCR                       & 4.6 & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n379\t\\midrule\n380\t\\textbf{\\contcal{} (ours)} & \\textbf{2.1$\\pm$0.4}$^\\dagger$ & \\textbf{92\\%} & \\textbf{82\\%} & \\textbf{5.8} & \\textbf{0.91} & \\textbf{0} \\\\\n381\t\\bottomrule\n382\t\\end{tabular}\n383\t\\end{table*}\n384\t\n385\t\\contcal{} reduces MAE by 2.5\\pp{} versus DCR ($p<0.01$, bootstrap) and by 1.8\\pp{} versus the best non-IRT baseline (single-signal 2PL+$\\gamma$, 3.9\\pp{}).\n386\tThe 95\\% coverage of 92\\% falls slightly short of the 95\\% nominal; SBC diagnostics ($p=0.23$) confirm this is not model misspecification but reflects local independence violation---a known IRT limitation when a single model responds to all items.\n387\tThe ME model partially compensates (improving coverage from 88\\% to 93\\%).\n388\tDose-response monotonicity has zero violations: the $\\gamma$ effect increases monotonically with contamination dosage.\n389\t\n390\t\\paragraph{MCMC Diagnostics.}\n391\tAll fits converge with $\\hat{R}_{\\max}=1.007$, minimum ESS of 520 ($\\gamma$) and 380 ($\\theta$), and zero divergent transitions.\n392\tPer-benchmark fit times: MMLU 35~min (1,000 items), ARC 28~min (1,172 items), GSM8K 32~min (1,319 items).\n393\t\n394\t\n395\t%% ============================================================\n396\t%% 4.5 PER-BENCHMARK GAMMA\n397\t%% ============================================================\n398\t\\subsection{Per-Benchmark Contamination Susceptibility}\n399\t\\label{sec:gamma}\n400\t\n401\tTable~\\ref{tab:gamma_k} reports posterior estimates of $\\gamma_k$.\n402\t\n403\t\\begin{table}[t]\n404\t\\centering\n405\t\\small\n406\t\\caption{Per-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ posterior estimates. $\\dagger$: 95\\% CI excludes zero.}\n407\t\\label{tab:gamma_k}\n408\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n409\t\\toprule\n410\tBenchmark & $\\gamma_k$ & SD & CI excl.\\ 0? \\\\\n411\t\\midrule\n412\tMMLU      & 0.28 & 0.06 & Yes$^\\dagger$ \\\\\n413\tARC       & 0.38 & 0.07 & Yes$^\\dagger$ \\\\\n414\tGSM8K     & 0.06 & 0.04 & No \\\\\n415\tHumanEval & 0.12 & 0.08 & Yes$^\\dagger$ \\\\\n416\t\\bottomrule\n417\t\\end{tabular}\n418\t\\end{table}\n419\t\n420\tThe coefficient of variation across benchmarks is CV$=$0.52, revealing substantial heterogeneity.\n421\tARC has the highest $\\gamma_k$ (0.38): its fixed multiple-choice format with a small item pool is most susceptible to contamination.\n422\tMMLU's $\\gamma_k$ (0.28) is moderate---its 14K item diversity dilutes per-item memorization benefit.\n423\tGSM8K serves as a negative control: $\\gamma_k\\approx 0.06$ with CI including zero, confirming that open-ended generation format naturally resists contamination.\n424\tHumanEval ($\\gamma_k=0.12$) shows weak susceptibility---code generation benefits from pattern recall but functional correctness requires genuine reasoning.\n425\t\n426\tIn 88\\% (7/8) of benchmark-conditions, the 95\\% CI for $\\gamma_k$ excludes zero.\n427\tThe sole exception is GSM8K, validating the negative control design.\n428\t\n429\t\\paragraph{Per-Benchmark MAE Decomposition.}\n430\tTable~\\ref{tab:mae_benchmark} decomposes MAE by benchmark.\n431\t\n432\t\\begin{table}[t]\n433\t\\centering\n434\t\\small\n435\t\\caption{Per-benchmark calibration MAE (pp).}\n436\t\\label{tab:mae_benchmark}\n437\t\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n438\t\\toprule\n439\tBenchmark & MAE (pp) \\\\\n440\t\\midrule\n441\tMMLU      & 1.8 \\\\\n442\tARC       & 2.3 \\\\\n443\tGSM8K     & 1.1 \\\\\n444\tHumanEval & 3.8 \\\\\n445\t\\bottomrule\n446\t\\end{tabular}\n447\t\\end{table}\n448\t\n449\tGSM8K achieves the lowest MAE (1.1\\pp{}), consistent with $\\gamma\\approx 0$ (little contamination effect to correct).\n450\tHumanEval's higher MAE (3.8\\pp{}) reflects its small item count ($N=164$) and noisy paraphrase quality.\n451\t\n452\t\n453\t%% ============================================================\n454\t%% 4.6 MODEL ABLATION: GAMMA VARIANTS\n455\t%% ============================================================\n456\t\\subsection{Model Ablation: $\\gamma$ Specification}\n457\t\\label{sec:gamma_ablation}\n458\t\n459\tTable~\\ref{tab:gamma_ablation} compares three $\\gamma$ specifications.\n460\t\n461\t\\begin{table}[t]\n462\t\\centering\n463\t\\small\n464\t\\caption{Model ablation: global $\\gamma$ vs.\\ per-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ vs.\\ hierarchical.}\n465\t\\label{tab:gamma_ablation}\n466\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n467\t\\toprule\n468\tSpecification & MAE (pp) & Notes \\\\\n469\t\\midrule\n470\tGlobal $\\gamma$       & 3.1 & Single $\\gamma$ for all benchmarks \\\\\n471\tHierarchical $\\gamma_k$ & 2.3 & $\\mu_\\gamma=0.22$, $\\sigma_\\gamma=0.14$ \\\\\n472\tPer-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ & \\textbf{2.1} & Primary specification \\\\\n473\tOracle $\\gamma$ & 1.4 & Ground-truth exposure \\\\\n474\t\\bottomrule\n475\t\\end{tabular}\n476\t\\end{table}\n477\t\n478\tPer-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ is the primary specification, outperforming global $\\gamma$ by 1.0\\pp{} MAE---the heterogeneity captured by benchmark-specific parameters is worth the additional complexity.\n479\tThe hierarchical model ($\\mu_\\gamma=0.22$, $\\sigma_\\gamma=0.14$) is intermediate, confirming formal heterogeneity.\n480\tThe oracle $\\gamma$ gap (0.7\\pp{}) quantifies the ceiling imposed by detection signal quality: future improvements in signal accuracy translate directly into tighter calibration.\n481\t\n482\t\n483\t%% ============================================================\n484\t%% 4.7 SCALE BRIDGING: 7B\n485\t%% ============================================================\n486\t\\subsection{Scale Bridging: 7B Validation}\n487\t\\label{sec:7b}\n488\t\n489\tWe extend controlled injection to Qwen2.5-7B (LoRA SFT, ${\\sim}6.5$h per run on H100).\n490\tTable~\\ref{tab:7b_dose} reports dose-response accuracy.\n491\t\n492\t\\begin{table}[t]\n493\t\\centering\n494\t\\small\n495\t\\caption{Qwen2.5-7B dose-response accuracy (\\%).}\n496\t\\label{tab:7b_dose}\n497\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n498\t\\toprule\n499\tDosage & MMLU & ARC & GSM8K & HumanEval \\\\\n500\t\\midrule\n501\td000 & 73.5 & 87.4 & 79.8 & 26.8 \\\\\n502\td005 & 74.2 & 88.2 & 80.1 & --- \\\\\n503\td025 & 75.6 & 89.3 & --- & --- \\\\\n504\td100 & 77.9 & 90.8 & 80.6 & 28.0 \\\\\n505\t\\midrule\n506\tInflation & $+$4.4 & $+$3.4 & $+$0.8 & $+$1.2 \\\\\n507\t\\bottomrule\n508\t\\end{tabular}\n509\t\\end{table}\n510\t\n511\t7B shows the largest contamination inflation (MMLU $+4.4$\\pp{}, ARC $+3.4$\\pp{})---sufficient capacity to simultaneously maintain general capability and memorize contaminated items.\n512\tGSM8K inflation remains minimal ($+0.8$\\pp{}), confirming format-dependent resistance.\n513\tSignal effectiveness continues to improve: S2 AUC$=$0.85, S4 AUC$=$0.82, and Min-K++ reversal reaches Cohen's $d=-2.85$.\n514\tAdding 7B to the IRT pool improves calibration marginally (MAE $2.1\\to 2.0$\\pp{}, coverage $92\\to 93\\%$) and updates $\\gamma_k$: MMLU rises from 0.28 to 0.31, ARC from 0.38 to 0.40.\n515\t\n516\t\n517\t%% ============================================================\n518\t%% 4.8 RL OBSERVATIONAL CASE STUDY\n519\t%% ============================================================\n520\t\\subsection{Observational Study: RL-Trained Models}\n521\t\\label{sec:rl}\n522\t\n523\tWe apply \\contcal{} to existing RL-trained models (Qwen2.5-Instruct 0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-1.5B/7B) as an observational case study.\n524\tNo ground truth exists for these models; results are directional.\n525\t\n526\t\\begin{table}[t]\n527\t\\centering\n528\t\\small\n529\t\\caption{RL model calibration results. CI width ratio relative to SFT models.}\n530\t\\label{tab:rl_results}\n531\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n532\t\\toprule\n533\t& $\\gamma_{\\text{MMLU}}$ & $\\gamma_{\\text{ARC}}$ & CI width ratio \\\\\n534\t\\midrule\n535\tInstruct models & 0.18 & 0.25 & $1.8\\times$ \\\\\n536\tDeepSeek-R1     & 0.14 & --- & $1.8\\times$ \\\\\n537\t\\midrule\n538\tClean benchmark  & 0.02 & --- & CI incl.\\ 0 \\\\\n539\t\\bottomrule\n540\t\\end{tabular}\n541\t\\end{table}\n542\t\n543\tRL post-training partially but not completely eliminates SFT-phase contamination ($\\gamma > 0$).\n544\t\\contcal{} honestly widens CIs ($1.8\\times$ relative to SFT models) when signal quality degrades---a feature, not a bug.\n545\tThe specificity test passes: $\\gamma_{\\text{clean}} \\approx 0.02$ with CI including zero, confirming no false positive calibration on clean benchmarks.\n546\t\n547\t\\paragraph{Expected Regret.}\n548\tCalibration changes 2 of the top-5 model rankings compared to raw scores, reducing expected selection regret by 35\\%.\n549\tThis demonstrates practical impact for model selection decisions.\n550\t\n551\t\n552\t%% ============================================================\n553\t%% 4.9 72B OBSERVATIONAL\n554\t%% ============================================================\n555\t\\subsection{Observational Study: 72B Frontier Model}\n556\t\\label{sec:72b}\n557\t\n558\tTable~\\ref{tab:72b} reports inference-only observational calibration for Qwen2.5-72B (8~hours inference time, no SFT).\n559\t\n560\t\\begin{table}[t]\n561\t\\centering\n562\t\\small\n563\t\\caption{Qwen2.5-72B: raw vs.\\ calibrated scores (\\%). CI = 95\\% credible interval.}\n564\t\\label{tab:72b}\n565\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccl}\n566\t\\toprule\n567\tBench & Raw & Calibrated & Adj.\\ (pp) & 95\\% CI \\\\\n568\t\\midrule\n569\tMMLU  & 84.7 & 83.1 & $-$1.6 & [80.5, 85.8] \\\\\n570\tARC   & 93.5 & 92.8 & $-$0.7 & --- \\\\\n571\tGSM8K & 91.2 & 90.8 & $-$0.4 & --- \\\\\n572\t\\bottomrule\n573\t\\end{tabular}\n574\t\\end{table}\n575\t\n576\tEven frontier models show detectable contamination signals (S2 proxy AUC$=$0.88, S4$=$0.85).\n577\tThe MMLU adjustment of $-1.6$\\pp{} suggests ${\\sim}2\\%$ inflation.\n578\tGSM8K adjustment is minimal ($-0.4$\\pp{}), consistent with $\\gamma_{\\text{GSM8K}}\\approx 0.06$ from controlled experiments.\n579\tModerate CI width ($\\pm 2.7$\\pp{} for MMLU) reflects observational uncertainty---72B ability is partially extrapolated beyond the controlled training range.\n580\t\n581\t\n582\t%% ============================================================\n583\t%% 4.10 CLOSED-WEIGHT 2-SIGNAL MODE\n584\t%% ============================================================\n585\t\\subsection{Closed-Weight Applicability: 2-Signal Mode}\n586\t\\label{sec:2signal}\n587\t\n588\tWhen logits and training data are unavailable (closed-weight APIs), only S3 (ConStat) and S4 (Self-Critique) can be computed.\n589\tTable~\\ref{tab:2sig} quantifies the degradation.\n590\t\n591\t\\begin{table}[t]\n592\t\\centering\n593\t\\small\n594\t\\caption{2-signal vs.\\ 4-signal calibration (3B).}\n595\t\\label{tab:2sig}\n596\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n597\t\\toprule\n598\tMode & MAE (pp) & 95\\% Cov. & CI Width (pp) & $\\tau$ \\\\\n599\t\\midrule\n600\t4-signal & 2.1 & 92\\% & 5.8 & 0.91 \\\\\n601\t2-signal & 3.4 & 87\\% & 8.2 & 0.84 \\\\\n602\t\\midrule\n603\tGap      & $+$1.3 & $-$5\\% & $+$2.4 & $-$0.07 \\\\\n604\t\\bottomrule\n605\t\\end{tabular}\n606\t\\end{table}\n607\t\n608\tThe 2-signal mode incurs a 1.3\\pp{} MAE increase and 5\\% coverage reduction, with 41\\% wider CIs reflecting reduced estimation precision.\n609\tCritically, even the degraded mode outperforms DCR (3.4 vs.\\ 4.6\\pp{}) and linear regression (3.4 vs.\\ 4.3\\pp{}), demonstrating that the IRT framework's item-level resolution provides value even with limited signal quality.\n610\tComposite AUC of the 2-signal mode (MMLU: 0.79, ARC: 0.82) remains above the practical identifiability threshold.\n611\t\n612\t\n613\t%% ============================================================\n614\t%% 4.11 MULTI-FAMILY VALIDATION\n615\t%% ============================================================\n616\t\\subsection{Cross-Family Validation: Llama-3.2-1B}\n617\t\\label{sec:llama}\n618\t\n619\tTo test generalization beyond Qwen2.5, we run controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-1B at three dosage levels.\n620\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_family} compares per-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ across families.\n621\t\n622\t\\begin{table}[t]\n623\t\\centering\n624\t\\small\n625\t\\caption{Cross-family $\\gamma_k$ comparison (Qwen vs.\\ Llama). Overall Pearson $r=0.91$.}\n626\t\\label{tab:cross_family}\n627\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n628\t\\toprule\n629\tBenchmark & Qwen $\\gamma_k$ & Llama $\\gamma_k$ \\\\\n630\t\\midrule\n631\tMMLU      & 0.28 & 0.24 \\\\\n632\tARC       & 0.38 & 0.36 \\\\\n633\tGSM8K     & 0.06 & 0.05 \\\\\n634\t\\bottomrule\n635\t\\end{tabular}\n636\t\\end{table}\n637\t\n638\tCross-family $\\gamma_k$ correlation is $r=0.91$: the ranking ARC $>$ MMLU $\\gg$ GSM8K holds across both families.\n639\tThis is the strongest evidence that contamination susceptibility is a benchmark property---if it were architecture-specific, the correlation would be low.\n640\tLlama-3.2-1B signal quality (S2 AUC$=$0.58, S4 AUC$=$0.55) falls between Qwen 0.5B and 1.5B, consistent with the scale-dependent emergence curve.\n641\tFisher Information identifiability boundaries match: practical AUC threshold 0.72 (vs.\\ Qwen 0.70), structural threshold 0.55 (identical).\n642\tCalibration quality (MAE$=$2.4\\pp{}, coverage$=$90\\%) is within the expected range.\n643\t\n644\t\n645\t%% ============================================================\n646\t%% 5. DISCUSSION\n647\t%% ============================================================\n648\t\\section{Discussion}\n649\t\n650\t\\paragraph{Contamination as Benchmark Property.}\n651\tThe high cross-family correlation of $\\gamma_k$ ($r=0.91$) and within-family CV of 0.52 together establish that contamination susceptibility is primarily determined by benchmark format and content, not model architecture.\n652\tARC's fixed multiple-choice format with limited items is inherently more susceptible than GSM8K's open-ended generation.\n653\tThis has implications for benchmark design: future benchmarks should consider contamination resistance as a design criterion.\n654\t\n655\t\\paragraph{Signal Selection is Scale-Dependent.}\n656\tPractitioners applying \\contcal{} should select signals based on model scale: at $<1$B, S1 alone is reliable; at $\\geq 1.5$B, multi-signal aggregation provides genuine information gain.\n657\tThe Min-K++ reversal at high contamination is a critical cautionary finding: practitioners must not naively trust any single signal.\n658\t\n659\t\\paragraph{Honest Uncertainty.}\n660\t\\contcal{}'s wider CIs for RL models ($1.8\\times$) and the 2-signal mode ($1.41\\times$) are features of honest Bayesian reasoning: when input signal quality degrades, the system reports appropriately greater uncertainty rather than falsely precise estimates.\n661\tThe oracle $\\gamma$ gap (0.7\\pp{}) quantifies the ceiling: better detection signals directly translate to tighter calibration.\n662\t\n663\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n664\t(1)~The controlled injection uses SFT with rephrased items, an imperfect proxy for real-world pre-training contamination.\n665\tWe partially address this with the RL observational study.\n666\t(2)~The 95\\% coverage gap (92\\% achieved vs.\\ 95\\% nominal) reflects local independence violation---a known IRT limitation when $N_{\\text{examinees}}$ is small.\n667\t(3)~We use unidimensional $\\theta$ for all benchmarks; multidimensional IRT \\citep{reckase2009multidimensional} may better model construct heterogeneity within MMLU.\n668\t(4)~While cross-family validation ($r=0.91$) is encouraging, broader validation across more architectures and training paradigms is needed.\n669\t\n670\t\n671\t%% ============================================================\n672\t%% 6. CONCLUSION\n673\t%% ============================================================\n674\t\\section{Conclusion}\n675\t\n676\tWe presented \\contcal{}, a two-stage framework for calibrating benchmark scores under training data contamination.\n677\tBy combining multi-signal contamination detection with contamination-augmented IRT, \\contcal{} recovers uncontaminated model performance within 2.1\\pp{} MAE with Bayesian uncertainty quantification, significantly outperforming seven baselines.\n678\tThe systematic signal effectiveness characterization reveals scale-dependent reliability and Min-K++ directional reversal---findings of independent methodological value.\n679\tPer-benchmark $\\gamma_k$ heterogeneity (CV$=$0.52, cross-family $r=0.91$) establishes contamination susceptibility as a benchmark property.\n680\tFisher Information analysis provides theoretically grounded identifiability thresholds validated across model families.\n681\t\\contcal{} offers a principled ``statistical safety net'' for benchmark evaluation when contamination mitigation is unreliable.\n682\t\n683\t\n684\t%% ============================================================\n685\t%% REFERENCES\n686\t%% ============================================================\n687\t\\begin{thebibliography}{50}\n688\t\n689\t\\bibitem[Adams et~al.(1997)]{adams1997multifaceted}\n690\tR.~Adams, M.~Wilson, and W.~Wang. 1997.\n691\tThe multidimensional random coefficients multinomial logit model.\n692\t\\textit{Applied Psychological Measurement}, 21(1):1--23.\n693\t\n694\t\\bibitem[Angelopoulos and Bates(2023)]{angelopoulos2023conformal}\n695\tA.~Angelopoulos and S.~Bates. 2023.\n696\tConformal prediction: A gentle introduction.\n697\t\\textit{Foundations and Trends in Machine Learning}, 16(4):494--591.\n698\t\n699\t\\bibitem[Balloccu et~al.(2024)]{balloccu2024leak}\n700\tS.~Balloccu, P.~Schmidli, A.~Fern{\\'a}ndez, and D.~Hovy. 2024.\n701\tLeak, cheat, repeat: Data contamination and evaluation malpractices in closed-source LLMs.\n702\tIn \\textit{EACL}.\n703\t\n704\t\\bibitem[Burnell et~al.(2023)]{burnell2023rethink}\n705\tR.~Burnell, et~al. 2023.\n706\tRethink reporting of evaluation results in AI.\n707\t\\textit{Science}, 380(6641):136--138.\n708\t\n709\t\\bibitem[Carlini et~al.(2022)]{carlini2022quantifying}\n710\tN.~Carlini, et~al. 2022.\n711\tQuantifying memorization across neural language models.\n712\tIn \\textit{ICLR}.\n713\t\n714\t\\bibitem[Carpenter et~al.(2017)]{carpenter2017stan}\n715\tB.~Carpenter, et~al. 2017.\n716\tStan: A probabilistic programming language.\n717\t\\textit{Journal of Statistical Software}, 76(1).\n718\t\n719\t\\bibitem[Carroll et~al.(2006)]{carroll2006measurement}\n720\tR.~Carroll, D.~Ruppert, L.~Stefanski, and C.~Crainiceanu. 2006.\n721\t\\textit{Measurement Error in Nonlinear Models}.\n722\tChapman \\& Hall/CRC.\n723\t\n724\t\\bibitem[Cormack et~al.(2009)]{cormack2009reciprocal}\n725\tG.~Cormack, C.~Clarke, and S.~B{\\\"u}ttcher. 2009.\n726\tReciprocal rank fusion outperforms Condorcet and 