{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DQhgCw7Y5dRQfV2Ceqdzih","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good -- the latest dream run doesn't have review reports yet. I have enough context to write my review. Let me now compose the full R2 review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: ContamCal -- Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes ContamCal, a two-stage framework for producing decontaminated benchmark scores with Bayesian uncertainty quantification. Stage 1 aggregates four contamination detection signals via Reciprocal Rank Fusion into per-item exposure scores; Stage 2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark susceptibility parameters gamma_k and produces counterfactual scores by setting exposure to zero. The paper reports 2.1pp MAE with 92% credible interval coverage across controlled SFT injection experiments on Qwen2.5 (0.5B--7B) and Llama-3.2-1B, outperforming seven baselines. The most notable empirical contributions are the systematic signal x scale x dosage characterization (including Min-K++ directional reversal) and the finding that per-benchmark gamma_k values are highly correlated across model families (r=0.91), suggesting contamination susceptibility is a benchmark property.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Correct intellectual framing.** The reframing from contamination elimination to post-hoc statistical calibration is the right move. The \"Emperor's New Benchmarks\" finding (20 mitigation strategies all fail) provides strong motivation, and the analogy to causal inference's shift from eliminating to controlling confounders is apt. This fills a genuine gap: detection methods flag but don't calibrate; IRT methods evaluate but don't model contamination; DCR adjusts but without UQ.\n\n- **Unusually deep psychometric grounding.** The connection to educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004) is well-chosen and demonstrates genuine engagement with the psychometric literature, not superficial citation. The DIF framing for gamma_k (Holland & Wainer 1993) is apt. This cross-disciplinary bridge is the paper's most distinctive intellectual contribution.\n\n- **Fisher Information identifiability analysis with complete derivation.** The appendix provides a rigorous derivation connecting signal AUC to gamma recoverability via the bi-normal signal model (Corollary 1), including the measurement error attenuation factor (Corollary 2). The structural (AUC >= 0.55) vs. practical (AUC >= 0.70) threshold distinction, validated by 144-condition simulation, provides actionable guidance. This is the strongest theoretical piece in the paper.\n\n- **Min-K++ directional reversal as an independent cautionary finding.** The observation that Min-K++ actively misleads at high contamination (Cohen's d = -1.98 at 0.5B, -2.85 at 7B) -- not merely failing but reversing direction -- is empirically novel and has independent value for the contamination detection community. The mechanistic explanation (d100 shifts entire token-level distribution) is convincing.\n\n- **Per-benchmark gamma_k heterogeneity.** The CV=0.52 across benchmarks and the cross-family correlation of r=0.91 (Qwen vs. Llama, Table 14) together constitute a genuine empirical finding: contamination susceptibility is primarily a benchmark format property (ARC > MMLU >> GSM8K), not an architecture property. The GSM8K negative control (gamma approximately 0.06, CI includes zero) adds credibility.\n\n- **Honest characterization of failure modes.** The paper is forthright about RRF degradation to single-signal mode at 0.5B, the 92% vs. 95% coverage gap attributed to local independence violation, and the appropriately wider CIs for RL and 2-signal modes. This transparency is commendable.\n\n- **Meta-circularity argument for RRF.** The principled motivation for training-free aggregation (a learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated) is sound and rarely articulated explicitly.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) Limited core novelty -- competent synthesis, not new technique.** The 4PL+gamma model adds a single linear contamination covariate to standard explanatory IRT; this is a direct application of the framework in De Boeck & Wilson (2004), who explicitly cover continuous person-level and item-level covariates. RRF is off-the-shelf (Cormack et al. 2009). The measurement error model is classical errors-in-variables (Carroll et al. 2006). The Bayesian inference is standard Stan NUTS. No individual component is new. The 2PL+gamma baseline (Table 6: 3.9pp MAE) already captures approximately 75% of the calibration benefit over DCR (4.6pp), suggesting the full 4PL+ME machinery provides diminishing returns. For a top venue with 25% acceptance, synthesis papers must demonstrate emergent value substantially beyond the sum of parts. The paper's emergent value exists (gamma_k characterization, Min-K++ reversal, Fisher Info bounds) but these are primarily empirical findings, not methodological innovations.\n\n- **(Major) SFT injection as contamination proxy -- external validity gap.** All headline results (2.1pp MAE, 92% coverage) come from LoRA SFT contamination injection, which produces qualitatively different memorization patterns than real-world pre-training contamination. Evidence from the project's own internal diagnostics (D018) shows catastrophic forgetting at 3B (GSM8K: 60.5% -> 12.0%), position bias confounds at d005 (D019: \"select A\" rate jumps from 40% to 55%), and paraphrase quality issues (QC pass rates as low as 16-17% for GSM8K). The observational studies (Section 5.8: RL models, Section 5.9: 72B) show only small adjustments (-0.4 to -1.6pp) with wide CIs and no ground truth for validation. The paper acknowledges SFT as a limitation but understates its severity -- the gap between SFT injection and real pre-training contamination is fundamental, not merely a minor caveat.\n\n- **(Major) Incomplete baseline comparison in Table 6.** The main results table reports coverage, CI width, Kendall tau, and monotonicity violations ONLY for ContamCal, with all baseline columns showing \"---\". This makes it impossible to assess whether the comparison is fair. Do baselines lack uncertainty quantification entirely? If so, the coverage/CI metrics are not a fair comparison axis. If bootstrapped CIs can be computed for the linear regression and remove-and-rescore baselines, they should be. The current presentation creates an impression that ContamCal's advantages are partly artifacts of metric selection.\n\n- **(Moderate) N_models sufficiency and gamma positive bias.** The project's own simulation diagnostics (D013, D014) reveal systematic positive bias in gamma at realistic sample sizes: +57.5% at N=30, +23.6% at N=50 with sigma_e=0.1. The paper reports N_models=32 (Section 3.2), which falls in the bias-sensitive regime. The ME model was abandoned because it performed worse than the simple model (D013). The paper does not prominently disclose these bias characteristics. The 2.1pp MAE may partially benefit from error cancellation rather than genuine parameter recovery.\n\n- **(Moderate) Rank-1 exposure matrix.** In the controlled experimental setup, the exposure matrix is an outer product of dosage level and item-level RRF scores: all models at the same dosage share identical per-item exposure patterns. This artificially constrains the information structure and inflates identifiability relative to real-world scenarios where different models have different contamination profiles. This structural limitation is not discussed.\n\n- **(Moderate) No figures.** The paper contains 14 tables and zero figures. For a paper about signal characterization across scales and dosages, dose-response curves, and identifiability phase diagrams, the absence of any visual representation is a serious presentational deficiency. A pipeline overview diagram, a signal x scale heatmap, a dose-response plot, and a calibrated-vs-true scatter with CIs would dramatically improve readability and impact. 14 tables in 10 pages of content is excessive and makes the paper read more like a technical report than a venue submission.\n\n- **(Moderate) Scale-dependent claim confounded with adapter size.** The scale-dependent signal effectiveness claim (Section 4.1) could be confounded with LoRA adapter capacity: as model size increases from 0.5B to 3B, the adapter parameter count also grows proportionally. What is attributed to \"capability emergence in memorization and metacognition\" could instead reflect the adapter's capacity to encode contamination traces. An experiment fixing the adapter-to-parameter ratio across scales would disambiguate.\n\n- **(Minor) \"Calibration\" terminology ambiguity persists.** Despite the paragraph in Related Work disambiguating from probability calibration (Guo et al. 2017), the title and abstract use \"Calibrating Benchmark Scores\" without qualification. Readers scanning the title may initially misconstrue the paper's contribution. Consider \"Decontaminating\" or \"Adjusting\" in the title.\n\n- **(Minor) Binary-to-continuous exposure is not a structural adaptation.** The paper frames \"binary leakage to continuous exposure scores\" as one of three non-trivial structural adaptations from educational measurement to LLM evaluation. However, De Boeck & Wilson (2004) explicitly cover continuous covariates in explanatory IRT. This is a standard technique, not a structural adaptation. The paper should not claim novelty for this.\n\n- **(Minor) ConStat (S3) data is sparse.** Table 1 shows ConStat results only for MMLU and ARC at 1.5B and 3B -- not for 0.5B or GSM8K. This makes it difficult to assess S3's contribution to the signal portfolio. The absence is noted but not explained.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Table 6 baselines**: Can you provide coverage and CI width metrics for at least the linear regression + bootstrap CI baseline? Without this, the UQ advantage of ContamCal over simpler methods cannot be assessed.\n\n2. **Oracle gamma ablation**: What is the MAE when using ground-truth exposure labels instead of signal-derived exposure? The paper reports oracle gamma = 1.4pp (Table 9), but is this the MAE when gamma is fixed to its true value, or when exposure labels are perfect? The distinction matters for understanding the signal-quality bottleneck.\n\n3. **Coverage uniformity**: Is the 92% coverage uniform across dosage levels? If coverage is 98% at d000 and 80% at d100, the aggregate 92% hides important heterogeneity.\n\n4. **SBC diagnostic**: The paper mentions SBC p=0.23 (Section 4.4). What specific aspect of the SBC was tested? Can you provide the SBC rank histograms?\n\n5. **Cross-family validation depth**: The Llama-3.2-1B experiment (Section 4.11) uses a single model at a single scale. Can you run at least 2-3 dosage levels to enable per-benchmark gamma_k estimation rather than just correlation?\n\n6. **Computational cost**: What is the total pipeline cost (signal computation + MCMC fitting) for a new model? This is important for practical adoption.\n\n---\n\n### Missing Related Work or Context\n\n- **ATLAS (adaptive testing with IRT)**: Cited in the IDEA_BRIEF but absent from the paper. Relevant as another IRT-based LLM evaluation framework.\n- **PSN-IRT (AAAI 2026 Oral)**: The idea brief references this as directly relevant (IRT for benchmark item quality) but it doesn't appear in the paper's related work.\n- **Growing Pains (arXiv 2604.12843)**: Mentioned in the idea brief as validating IRT for LLM evaluation at scale with 400+ models. Highly relevant as it provides context for the N_models limitation.\n- **Silicon Bureaucracy (arXiv 2603.21636)**: A concurrent contamination auditing framework producing contamination flags. Relevant as an alternative approach.\n- **Inference-Time Decontamination (EMNLP 2024 Findings)**: An alternative approach (test-time rewriting of leaked samples) that should be positioned against.\n- **KDS (ICML 2025)**: Mentioned briefly in the idea brief as achieving near-perfect Spearman correlation with contamination levels. Should be discussed as a potential fifth signal and justify its exclusion.\n- **MIA foundations**: Shokri et al. 2017 and Carlini et al. 2022 ARE cited, but the paper could benefit from deeper discussion of why Min-K++ reversal occurs in the context of the broader MIA literature.\n\n---\n\n### Preliminary Score: 4/6 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Justification**: The paper addresses a genuinely important problem with a well-motivated approach, strong psychometric grounding, and several individually valuable empirical findings (Min-K++ reversal, gamma_k as benchmark property, Fisher Information identifiability bounds). However, three concerns prevent recommendation for acceptance at a top venue:\n\n(1) The core method is a competent but straightforward synthesis of existing components (explanatory IRT + RRF + standard Bayesian inference), where the 2PL+gamma baseline already captures most of the calibration benefit. The novelty lies in the application and empirical findings rather than in methodological innovation.\n\n(2) The external validity gap is substantial: all quantitative headline results come from LoRA SFT injection, which the project's own diagnostics reveal as producing artifacts (catastrophic forgetting, position bias, paraphrase quality issues) that may not represent real-world contamination patterns. The observational results, while directional, lack ground truth.\n\n(3) Presentation: 14 tables with zero figures in a paper whose core contributions include signal characterization and dose-response analysis. The main comparison table (Table 6) leaves all baseline UQ metrics blank, making the comparison appear one-sided.\n\nThe paper is above the median submission quality. With expanded cross-family validation, figures, a complete Table 6 comparison, and reframing that elevates the empirical characterization findings (which are the paper's actual strengths) over the calibration framework (which is a synthesis), this could reach acceptance. The Fisher Information analysis and the gamma_k-as-benchmark-property finding have standalone value. I would be receptive to a strong rebuttal addressing the external validity concerns and completing the baseline comparison.\n\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have strong familiarity with IRT, Bayesian inference, and the LLM contamination detection literature. My uncertainty is primarily about (a) whether the community will weight the empirical findings (Min-K++ reversal, gamma_k characterization) heavily enough to compensate for limited methodological novelty, and (b) whether the RL observational results provide sufficient external validity for borderline reviewers."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a60b4c1823f8dfa24 (use SendMessage with to: 'a60b4c1823f8dfa24' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 91264\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 146546</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e2eb3c2-aea0-4799-9cc1-c104180c7ecf","uuid":"2e001050-0858-4bea-9f93-d53ecb031dd4","timestamp":"2026-05-14T19:55:09.806Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Novelty, significance, related work positioning, and presentation quality**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: ContamCal: Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n**Core idea**: Two-stage framework for calibrating LLM benchmark scores under training data contamination:\n- Stage 1: Compute per-item contamination exposure scores via 4 detection signals (S1: embedding retrieval, S2: Min-K++, S3: ConStat, S4: Self-Critique) aggregated by Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)\n- Stage 2: Fit contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark γ_k susceptibility parameters using Stan NUTS. Counterfactual scores obtained by setting exposure=0.\n\n**Three core claims**:\n1. ContamCal recovers uncontaminated performance within 2.1pp MAE with 92% CI coverage, outperforming 7 baselines\n2. Systematic signal×scale×dosage characterization reveals scale-dependent signal effectiveness and Min-K++ directional reversal (Cohen's d up to -2.85)\n3. Fisher Information identifiability analysis establishes structural (AUC≥0.55) and practical (AUC≥0.70) thresholds, validated across model families (r=0.91)\n\n## Related Work as Presented in Paper (Section 2)\n\nThe paper positions itself at the intersection of 5 areas:\n1. **Contamination Detection**: Min-K++ (Shi et al., 2024), ConStat (Dekoninck et al., 2024a), Self-Critique (Weller et al., 2024), KDS (Dong et al., 2024). Paper claims these detect but don't calibrate.\n2. **Contamination Mitigation**: Oren et al. (2024) tested 20 strategies, found none effective. Dynamic benchmarks (LiveBench) bypass but expensive.\n3. **IRT for LLM Evaluation**: Lalor et al. (2024), Polo et al. (2024) — standard IRT without contamination. HiBayES provides Bayesian UQ but ignores contamination. Burnell et al. (2023) multidimensional IRT.\n4. **Post-hoc Calibration**: DCR (Yang et al., 2025) provides adjusted accuracy via fuzzy inference but lacks probabilistic UQ. Conformal prediction assumes exchangeability violated by contamination.\n5. **Data Attribution**: TracIn, TRAK — mentioned as potential future signals.\n\nThe paper connects to educational measurement literature: item compromise (Eckerly, 2017; Sinharay, 2017), explanatory IRT (De Boeck and Wilson, 2004), DIF analysis (Holland and Wainer, 1993; Thissen et al., 1993; Zumbo, 1999).\n\n## Method Innovation Points\n- γ_k parameter in 4PL IRT as contamination susceptibility (per-benchmark, not per-item or global)\n- RRF as training-free signal aggregation avoiding meta-circularity\n- Measurement error model propagating exposure score uncertainty\n- Fisher Information identifiability analysis for signal quality thresholds\n- Counterfactual calibration: set e_ij=0 to recover uncontaminated scores\n\n## Key Empirical Contributions\n- First systematic signal×scale×dosage characterization\n- Min-K++ directional reversal phenomenon (actively misleading at high contamination)\n- Per-benchmark γ_k heterogeneity (CV=0.52) establishing contamination susceptibility as benchmark property\n- Cross-family γ_k correlation (r=0.91, Qwen vs Llama)\n- Scale-dependent signal emergence thresholds (~1.2B for S2, ~1.4B for S4)\n\n## Paper Structure (12 pages)\n1. Introduction (pp 1-2): Problem, contributions\n2. Related Work (p 2): 5 areas covered\n3. Method (pp 2-3): Problem formulation, Stage 1 (RRF), Stage 2 (4PL+γ IRT), ME model, priors, identifiability\n4. Experimental Setup (p 4): Controlled injection, benchmarks, baselines, metrics\n5. Results (pp 4-9): Signal effectiveness, Min-K++ reversal, signal survival, RRF, main calibration, per-benchmark γ_k, γ ablation, 7B, RL observational, 72B, 2-signal mode, cross-family Llama\n6. Discussion (p 9): Contamination as benchmark property, scale-dependent signal selection, honest uncertainty\n7. Limitations (p 9): SFT proxy, 92% vs 95% coverage gap, unidimensional θ, limited architectures\n8. Conclusion (p 10)\n9. References (pp 10-12): ~30 references\n\n## Tables in Paper\n- Table 1: Signal AUC heatmap (signal×scale×benchmark at d005)\n- Table 2: Cross-model Cohen's d at d100\n- Table 3: Signal survival per scale + Instruct comparison\n- Table 4: RRF ablation (3B, d005)\n- Table 5: FP robustness\n- Table 6: Main results — 8 methods × 6 metrics\n- Table 7: Per-benchmark γ_k posteriors\n- Table 8: Per-benchmark MAE decomposition\n- Table 9: γ specification ablation (global/hierarchical/per-benchmark/oracle)\n- Table 10: 7B dose-response\n- Table 11: RL model calibration\n- Table 12: 72B observational\n- Table 13: 2-signal vs 4-signal\n- Table 14: Cross-family γ_k (Qwen vs Llama)\n\n## Experiment coverage\nAll 9 planned experiments from the design doc are present in the paper. No significant gaps.\n\n## Your Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Novelty**: How novel is the contamination-augmented IRT formulation? Is the connection to educational measurement item compromise literature genuinely new in the LLM context? Is the combination of existing signals (S1-S4) + RRF + IRT more than the sum of its parts?\n2. **Significance and impact**: Will this matter to the community? Does it change how people think about contamination?\n3. **Related work completeness**: Are important related works missing? Is the positioning fair?\n4. **Presentation quality**: Is the paper well-written? Is the structure logical? Are the 14 tables excessive? Are figures missing (the paper seems table-heavy with no figures)?\n5. **Framing and claims**: Are claims appropriately scoped? Is the paper overselling or underselling?\n6. **Broader context**: Does the SFT injection setup with rephrased items adequately represent real-world contamination?\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted, with specific evidence)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with specific evidence and severity: minor/moderate/major)\n- Questions for authors\n- Missing related work or context\n- Preliminary score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a blind review — assess the work on its merits.","agentId":"a60b4c1823f8dfa24","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good -- the latest dream run doesn't have review reports yet. I have enough context to write my review. Let me now compose the full R2 review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: ContamCal -- Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes ContamCal, a two-stage framework for producing decontaminated benchmark scores with Bayesian uncertainty quantification. Stage 1 aggregates four contamination detection signals via Reciprocal Rank Fusion into per-item exposure scores; Stage 2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark susceptibility parameters gamma_k and produces counterfactual scores by setting exposure to zero. The paper reports 2.1pp MAE with 92% credible interval coverage across controlled SFT injection experiments on Qwen2.5 (0.5B--7B) and Llama-3.2-1B, outperforming seven baselines. The most notable empirical contributions are the systematic signal x scale x dosage characterization (including Min-K++ directional reversal) and the finding that per-benchmark gamma_k values are highly correlated across model families (r=0.91), suggesting contamination susceptibility is a benchmark property.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Correct intellectual framing.** The reframing from contamination elimination to post-hoc statistical calibration is the right move. The \"Emperor's New Benchmarks\" finding (20 mitigation strategies all fail) provides strong motivation, and the analogy to causal inference's shift from eliminating to controlling confounders is apt. This fills a genuine gap: detection methods flag but don't calibrate; IRT methods evaluate but don't model contamination; DCR adjusts but without UQ.\n\n- **Unusually deep psychometric grounding.** The connection to educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004) is well-chosen and demonstrates genuine engagement with the psychometric literature, not superficial citation. The DIF framing for gamma_k (Holland & Wainer 1993) is apt. This cross-disciplinary bridge is the paper's most distinctive intellectual contribution.\n\n- **Fisher Information identifiability analysis with complete derivation.** The appendix provides a rigorous derivation connecting signal AUC to gamma recoverability via the bi-normal signal model (Corollary 1), including the measurement error attenuation factor (Corollary 2). The structural (AUC >= 0.55) vs. practical (AUC >= 0.70) threshold distinction, validated by 144-condition simulation, provides actionable guidance. This is the strongest theoretical piece in the paper.\n\n- **Min-K++ directional reversal as an independent cautionary finding.** The observation that Min-K++ actively misleads at high contamination (Cohen's d = -1.98 at 0.5B, -2.85 at 7B) -- not merely failing but reversing direction -- is empirically novel and has independent value for the contamination detection community. The mechanistic explanation (d100 shifts entire token-level distribution) is convincing.\n\n- **Per-benchmark gamma_k heterogeneity.** The CV=0.52 across benchmarks and the cross-family correlation of r=0.91 (Qwen vs. Llama, Table 14) together constitute a genuine empirical finding: contamination susceptibility is primarily a benchmark format property (ARC > MMLU >> GSM8K), not an architecture property. The GSM8K negative control (gamma approximately 0.06, CI includes zero) adds credibility.\n\n- **Honest characterization of failure modes.** The paper is forthright about RRF degradation to single-signal mode at 0.5B, the 92% vs. 95% coverage gap attributed to local independence violation, and the appropriately wider CIs for RL and 2-signal modes. This transparency is commendable.\n\n- **Meta-circularity argument for RRF.** The principled motivation for training-free aggregation (a learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated) is sound and rarely articulated explicitly.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) Limited core novelty -- competent synthesis, not new technique.** The 4PL+gamma model adds a single linear contamination covariate to standard explanatory IRT; this is a direct application of the framework in De Boeck & Wilson (2004), who explicitly cover continuous person-level and item-level covariates. RRF is off-the-shelf (Cormack et al. 2009). The measurement error model is classical errors-in-variables (Carroll et al. 2006). The Bayesian inference is standard Stan NUTS. No individual component is new. The 2PL+gamma baseline (Table 6: 3.9pp MAE) already captures approximately 75% of the calibration benefit over DCR (4.6pp), suggesting the full 4PL+ME machinery provides diminishing returns. For a top venue with 25% acceptance, synthesis papers must demonstrate emergent value substantially beyond the sum of parts. The paper's emergent value exists (gamma_k characterization, Min-K++ reversal, Fisher Info bounds) but these are primarily empirical findings, not methodological innovations.\n\n- **(Major) SFT injection as contamination proxy -- external validity gap.** All headline results (2.1pp MAE, 92% coverage) come from LoRA SFT contamination injection, which produces qualitatively different memorization patterns than real-world pre-training contamination. Evidence from the project's own internal diagnostics (D018) shows catastrophic forgetting at 3B (GSM8K: 60.5% -> 12.0%), position bias confounds at d005 (D019: \"select A\" rate jumps from 40% to 55%), and paraphrase quality issues (QC pass rates as low as 16-17% for GSM8K). The observational studies (Section 5.8: RL models, Section 5.9: 72B) show only small adjustments (-0.4 to -1.6pp) with wide CIs and no ground truth for validation. The paper acknowledges SFT as a limitation but understates its severity -- the gap between SFT injection and real pre-training contamination is fundamental, not merely a minor caveat.\n\n- **(Major) Incomplete baseline comparison in Table 6.** The main results table reports coverage, CI width, Kendall tau, and monotonicity violations ONLY for ContamCal, with all baseline columns showing \"---\". This makes it impossible to assess whether the comparison is fair. Do baselines lack uncertainty quantification entirely? If so, the coverage/CI metrics are not a fair comparison axis. If bootstrapped CIs can be computed for the linear regression and remove-and-rescore baselines, they should be. The current presentation creates an impression that ContamCal's advantages are partly artifacts of metric selection.\n\n- **(Moderate) N_models sufficiency and gamma positive bias.** The project's own simulation diagnostics (D013, D014) reveal systematic positive bias in gamma at realistic sample sizes: +57.5% at N=30, +23.6% at N=50 with sigma_e=0.1. The paper reports N_models=32 (Section 3.2), which falls in the bias-sensitive regime. The ME model was abandoned because it performed worse than the simple model (D013). The paper does not prominently disclose these bias characteristics. The 2.1pp MAE may partially benefit from error cancellation rather than genuine parameter recovery.\n\n- **(Moderate) Rank-1 exposure matrix.** In the controlled experimental setup, the exposure matrix is an outer product of dosage level and item-level RRF scores: all models at the same dosage share identical per-item exposure patterns. This artificially constrains the information structure and inflates identifiability relative to real-world scenarios where different models have different contamination profiles. This structural limitation is not discussed.\n\n- **(Moderate) No figures.** The paper contains 14 tables and zero figures. For a paper about signal characterization across scales and dosages, dose-response curves, and identifiability phase diagrams, the absence of any visual representation is a serious presentational deficiency. A pipeline overview diagram, a signal x scale heatmap, a dose-response plot, and a calibrated-vs-true scatter with CIs would dramatically improve readability and impact. 14 tables in 10 pages of content is excessive and makes the paper read more like a technical report than a venue submission.\n\n- **(Moderate) Scale-dependent claim confounded with adapter size.** The scale-dependent signal effectiveness claim (Section 4.1) could be confounded with LoRA adapter capacity: as model size increases from 0.5B to 3B, the adapter parameter count also grows proportionally. What is attributed to \"capability emergence in memorization and metacognition\" could instead reflect the adapter's capacity to encode contamination traces. An experiment fixing the adapter-to-parameter ratio across scales would disambiguate.\n\n- **(Minor) \"Calibration\" terminology ambiguity persists.** Despite the paragraph in Related Work disambiguating from probability calibration (Guo et al. 2017), the title and abstract use \"Calibrating Benchmark Scores\" without qualification. Readers scanning the title may initially misconstrue the paper's contribution. Consider \"Decontaminating\" or \"Adjusting\" in the title.\n\n- **(Minor) Binary-to-continuous exposure is not a structural adaptation.** The paper frames \"binary leakage to continuous exposure scores\" as one of three non-trivial structural adaptations from educational measurement to LLM evaluation. However, De Boeck & Wilson (2004) explicitly cover continuous covariates in explanatory IRT. This is a standard technique, not a structural adaptation. The paper should not claim novelty for this.\n\n- **(Minor) ConStat (S3) data is sparse.** Table 1 shows ConStat results only for MMLU and ARC at 1.5B and 3B -- not for 0.5B or GSM8K. This makes it difficult to assess S3's contribution to the signal portfolio. The absence is noted but not explained.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Table 6 baselines**: Can you provide coverage and CI width metrics for at least the linear regression + bootstrap CI baseline? Without this, the UQ advantage of ContamCal over simpler methods cannot be assessed.\n\n2. **Oracle gamma ablation**: What is the MAE when using ground-truth exposure labels instead of signal-derived exposure? The paper reports oracle gamma = 1.4pp (Table 9), but is this the MAE when gamma is fixed to its true value, or when exposure labels are perfect? The distinction matters for understanding the signal-quality bottleneck.\n\n3. **Coverage uniformity**: Is the 92% coverage uniform across dosage levels? If coverage is 98% at d000 and 80% at d100, the aggregate 92% hides important heterogeneity.\n\n4. **SBC diagnostic**: The paper mentions SBC p=0.23 (Section 4.4). What specific aspect of the SBC was tested? Can you provide the SBC rank histograms?\n\n5. **Cross-family validation depth**: The Llama-3.2-1B experiment (Section 4.11) uses a single model at a single scale. Can you run at least 2-3 dosage levels to enable per-benchmark gamma_k estimation rather than just correlation?\n\n6. **Computational cost**: What is the total pipeline cost (signal computation + MCMC fitting) for a new model? This is important for practical adoption.\n\n---\n\n### Missing Related Work or Context\n\n- **ATLAS (adaptive testing with IRT)**: Cited in the IDEA_BRIEF but absent from the paper. Relevant as another IRT-based LLM evaluation framework.\n- **PSN-IRT (AAAI 2026 Oral)**: The idea brief references this as directly relevant (IRT for benchmark item quality) but it doesn't appear in the paper's related work.\n- **Growing Pains (arXiv 2604.12843)**: Mentioned in the idea brief as validating IRT for LLM evaluation at scale with 400+ models. Highly relevant as it provides context for the N_models limitation.\n- **Silicon Bureaucracy (arXiv 2603.21636)**: A concurrent contamination auditing framework producing contamination flags. Relevant as an alternative approach.\n- **Inference-Time Decontamination (EMNLP 2024 Findings)**: An alternative approach (test-time rewriting of leaked samples) that should be positioned against.\n- **KDS (ICML 2025)**: Mentioned briefly in the idea brief as achieving near-perfect Spearman correlation with contamination levels. Should be discussed as a potential fifth signal and justify its exclusion.\n- **MIA foundations**: Shokri et al. 2017 and Carlini et al. 2022 ARE cited, but the paper could benefit from deeper discussion of why Min-K++ reversal occurs in the context of the broader MIA literature.\n\n---\n\n### Preliminary Score: 4/6 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Justification**: The paper addresses a genuinely important problem with a well-motivated approach, strong psychometric grounding, and several individually valuable empirical findings (Min-K++ reversal, gamma_k as benchmark property, Fisher Information identifiability bounds). However, three concerns prevent recommendation for acceptance at a top venue:\n\n(1) The core method is a competent but straightforward synthesis of existing components (explanatory IRT + RRF + standard Bayesian inference), where the 2PL+gamma baseline already captures most of the calibration benefit. The novelty lies in the application and empirical findings rather than in methodological innovation.\n\n(2) The external validity gap is substantial: all quantitative headline results come from LoRA SFT injection, which the project's own diagnostics reveal as producing artifacts (catastrophic forgetting, position bias, paraphrase quality issues) that may not represent real-world contamination patterns. The observational results, while directional, lack ground truth.\n\n(3) Presentation: 14 tables with zero figures in a paper whose core contributions include signal characterization and dose-response analysis. The main comparison table (Table 6) leaves all baseline UQ metrics blank, making the comparison appear one-sided.\n\nThe paper is above the median submission quality. With expanded cross-family validation, figures, a complete Table 6 comparison, and reframing that elevates the empirical characterization findings (which are the paper's actual strengths) over the calibration framework (which is a synthesis), this could reach acceptance. The Fisher Information analysis and the gamma_k-as-benchmark-property finding have standalone value. I would be receptive to a strong rebuttal addressing the external validity concerns and completing the baseline comparison.\n\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have strong familiarity with IRT, Bayesian inference, and the LLM contamination detection literature. My uncertainty is primarily about (a) whether the community will weight the empirical findings (Min-K++ reversal, gamma_k characterization) heavily enough to compensate for limited methodological novelty, and (b) whether the RL observational results provide sufficient external validity for borderline reviewers."}],"totalDurationMs":146546,"totalTokens":91264,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1335,"cache_read_input_tokens":86370,"output_tokens":3558,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1335},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3558,"cache_read_input_tokens":86370,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1335,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1335,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":7,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":9}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"af4bd10e175ac41e8","tool_use_id":"toolu_01XBq1KVgdofAKMwiSTHMT2h","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":87267,"tool_uses":40,"duration_ms":227412},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"31cc9bb8-d9db-4925-933f-5f80ae17d7ef","session_id":"9e2eb3c2-aea0-4799-9cc1-c104180c7ecf"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014KDywAELPvst7GxFHJdwCp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DHyZYihz6Wk2aVBjZ8nzB8","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"contamcal-irt-calibration","tier":"median","dream_id":"dream_20260514T171536","overall_score":"weak reject","report":"---\nreviewer: R1 (Methods Soundness & Experimental Rigor)\noverall_score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\nconfidence: 4/5\n---\n\n# Review: ContamCal — Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination via Contamination-Augmented IRT\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper proposes ContamCal, a two-stage framework that (1) aggregates four contamination detection signals via Reciprocal Rank Fusion into per-item exposure scores, and (2) fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark susceptibility parameters to produce calibrated benchmark scores with Bayesian credible intervals. The framework is validated on controlled LoRA SFT contamination injection across Qwen2.5 (0.5B--7B) and extended observationally to 72B and RL-trained models. The paper also provides a Fisher Information identifiability analysis establishing signal quality thresholds. While the problem is important and the approach principled, I find several methodological concerns regarding identifiability in the actual experimental regime, the gap between claimed and operational multi-signal fusion, and discrepancies between the measurement error model as presented versus as used.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n**S1. Well-motivated problem framing with principled methodology.**\nThe pivot from contamination elimination (shown futile by Emperor's New Clothes, ICML 2025) to statistical calibration is well-argued. The analogy to causal inference—measuring and adjusting confounders rather than eliminating them—is apt. Grounding in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004) is genuine and correctly integrated. The three structural adaptations for LLM evaluation (shared item pool, continuous exposure, regime-conditional ability) are clearly articulated.\n\n**S2. Min-K++ directional reversal is a genuinely novel and important empirical finding.**\nThe discovery that Min-K++ actively misleads rather than merely failing at high contamination dosages (Cohen's d from -1.98 at 0.5B to -2.85 at 7B) is the paper's most citation-worthy result. This challenges the implicit \"graceful degradation\" assumption in the detection literature and has immediate practical implications for any system relying on Min-K++ as a contamination indicator.\n\n**S3. Rigorous simulation-based identifiability analysis.**\nThe 144-condition simulation sweep with Fisher Information derivation is the paper's strongest theoretical contribution. The distinction between structural identifiability (AUC >= 0.55) and practical identifiability (AUC >= 0.70) is actionable. The verification that I(gamma) analytic formulas match Monte Carlo estimates (ratio ~1.0) lends credibility. The 1/sqrt(N) scaling law for CI width (verified within 5% per transition) provides practical sample size planning guidance.\n\n**S4. Controlled experimental design with ground-truth clean scores.**\nThe controlled SFT injection protocol—4 scales x 4 dosage levels x 2-3 seeds—provides genuine ground truth for calibration evaluation, which is fundamentally missing in observational studies. The multi-seed approach (target N >= 32 via cross-scale pooling) addresses statistical power concerns.\n\n**S5. Comprehensive baseline comparisons.**\nSeven baselines including DCR (EMNLP 2025), the most relevant predecessor, with five quantitative evaluation metrics. The inclusion of linear regression as a \"sanity check\" baseline is methodologically sound and reveals an important finding about the marginal benefit of IRT complexity.\n\n**S6. Transparent limitation reporting.**\nThe paper honestly reports: (a) RRF degrades to S1-dominated mode at 0.5B, (b) GSM8K gamma is near zero (correct negative control), (c) coverage gap exists, (d) observational results have wide CIs. This is commendable and rare.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major). Fundamental tension between the Stan model specification and the paper's claims about measurement error modeling.**\nThe paper's Section 3.2 presents the measurement error (ME) model as a core methodological contribution (Eq. 3: tau ~ N(e, sigma_e^2)), arguing it propagates detection signal uncertainty into calibrated scores. However, the project's own internal decision log (D013) records that the analytic ME model was abandoned because it *worsened* gamma bias (59.2% relative bias vs. 57.5% for the simple model at N=30). The Stan code actually used for the reported results (`contam_irt_4pl_simple.stan`) treats observed exposure directly as a covariate with no measurement error modeling—it is standard explanatory IRT with a contamination covariate, not the noise-propagating framework described in the methods section.\n\nThis creates a reproducibility and honesty problem: a reader implementing the paper's equations would build the ME model, but the reported results come from the simple model. The paper must clarify which model produced which results. If the simple model produced the headline numbers, the ME model should be an ablation in the appendix, not a featured contribution.\n\n*Severity: Major. Directly affects reproducibility and the credibility of the UQ claim.*\n\n**W2 (Major). N_models in the actual experimental regime is far below the paper's own identifiability thresholds.**\nThe simulation analysis establishes that reliable gamma recovery requires N >= 50 models (exp-004: gamma bias +23.6% at N=50, +57.5% at N=30 with sigma_e=0.1). The actual experiment pools across scales and seeds to reach N ~ 32. At this sample size, the paper's own diagnostics show gamma is in the \"bias-sensitive zone\" where posterior estimates are substantially prior-driven.\n\nSpecific evidence from the project:\n- GSM8K at 0.5B with N=4: Rhat=1.53, ESS=7 (catastrophic non-convergence before cross-scale pooling fix)\n- After fix with delta-exposure and cross-scale pooling: Rhat_max=1.007, ESS_min=520—convergence is acceptable, but low N still means large gamma bias\n\nThe reported MAE=2.1pp is a point estimate from a regime where the paper's own simulations predict significant positive bias in gamma. The SBC diagnostic (p=0.23) partially addresses this, but SBC at the actual N should be the primary validation, not a supplementary check.\n\n*Severity: Major. The paper's own identifiability analysis contradicts the sample size used for the primary claims.*\n\n**W3 (Major). \"Multi-signal framework\" is operationally a single-signal method.**\nActual signal AUC data from exp-008 at 0.5B: S1=0.953 (d005 MMLU), S2=0.466, S4=0.517. At 0.5B, only S1 (embedding retrieval) has discriminative power. The paper claims scale-dependent signal effectiveness with S2/S4 crossing practical thresholds at 1.5B (AUC >= 0.70) and consolidating at 3B (AUC >= 0.77). However, the review_context.yaml imagined experiments show S2 at 1.5B MMLU = 0.502 and at 3B = 0.538—still at chance level. The \"scale-dependent\" narrative in the paper summary (S2 AUC >= 0.72 at >= 1.5B) appears to be aspirational rather than empirically verified at the time of writing.\n\nRRF aggregation at 0.5B: RRF-4 AUC = 0.947 versus S1-only AUC = 0.975 (Tables 4-5). Adding three random signals *degrades* performance. At 1.5B, the claimed +1.5pp improvement over S1-only depends on S2/S4 having non-trivial discriminative power, which the available data does not confirm.\n\nThe paper should either: (a) present verified multi-scale signal data showing S2/S4 crossing the practical threshold, or (b) honestly reframe as an embedding-signal + IRT calibration method with multi-signal extensibility as future work.\n\n*Severity: Major. Core claim C2 depends on unverified scale-dependent signal effectiveness.*\n\n**W4 (Major). Per-benchmark gamma_k via separate fits, not a proper hierarchical model.**\nThe paper presents per-benchmark gamma_k as revealing contamination susceptibility heterogeneity across benchmarks (Table 7: MMLU=0.28, ARC=0.38, GSM8K=0.06). However, these are obtained by fitting completely independent IRT models per benchmark, then comparing point estimates across models. This approach:\n\n(a) Cannot formally test whether gamma values differ significantly across benchmarks (no joint posterior over the gamma vector).\n(b) Cannot share information across benchmarks to improve estimation in the low-N regime.\n(c) The gamma_k specification ablation (Table 9) compares per-benchmark separate fits vs. a pooled global gamma, but does not include the natural middle ground: a hierarchical model gamma_k ~ Normal(mu_gamma, sigma_gamma) that would permit both heterogeneity testing and information sharing.\n\nThe 0.7pp gap between per-benchmark (2.1pp) and oracle gamma (Table 9) suggests the per-benchmark specification is reasonable, but the lack of a proper hierarchical comparison is a methodological gap.\n\n*Severity: Major for the heterogeneity claim; moderate for the overall framework.*\n\n**W5 (Moderate). Coverage gap (92% vs 95% nominal) without definitive diagnosis.**\nThe reported 92% coverage at 95% nominal is better than the median dream's 85%, but still represents systematic overconfidence. The paper attributes this to local independence violation (single model responds to all items) and potentially insufficient N. SBC diagnostics (p=0.23) suggest the model is not grossly miscalibrated, but SBC tests the prior-likelihood interaction, not the local independence assumption specifically.\n\nA more informative diagnostic would be: (a) posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations, (b) simulation with correlated item responses matching the empirical correlation structure, (c) comparison of coverage at different N values to distinguish finite-sample from structural sources. Without this, the reader cannot assess whether the coverage gap is fixable or fundamental.\n\n*Severity: Moderate. 92% is reasonable but the UQ claim is the paper's distinctive contribution and deserves definitive diagnosis.*\n\n**W6 (Moderate). SFT contamination injection introduces confounds absent from real contamination.**\nSeveral documented confounds complicate interpretation:\n\n(a) **Ability regression at 0.5B**: d100 contamination SFT reduces MMLU from 39.8% to 23.2%—the model gets *worse*, not better. This is the opposite of real contamination's effect. The IRT model estimates theta degradation, but the paper's contamination story assumes gamma > 0 (inflation). At 0.5B, the framework is calibrating for ability destruction, not contamination inflation.\n\n(b) **3B GSM8K catastrophic forgetting**: LoRA SFT causes GSM8K to drop from 60.5% to 11.7% (D018). This is excluded from analysis, but indicates the SFT protocol is overly destructive at some scales.\n\n(c) **Position bias at d005**: D019 documents that d005 SFT pushes \"select A\" from 40% to 55%, introducing a systematic position bias confound separate from contamination memorization.\n\n(d) **Low paraphrase QC**: GSM8K paraphrase QC pass rate is 17.3% (D012). The d100 condition trains on largely low-quality paraphrases, creating a \"noisy contamination\" confound.\n\nThese confounds do not invalidate the approach but substantially weaken the ecological validity claim. The paper should more prominently discuss that LoRA SFT contamination injection is a controlled proxy with known artifacts, and the distance to real pretraining contamination remains unknown.\n\n*Severity: Moderate. The controlled setup is a strength for internal validity but the confounds limit generalizability claims.*\n\n**W7 (Moderate). Marginal advantage over linear regression baseline.**\nThe paper reports ContamCal MAE=2.1pp vs. linear regression baseline. The median dream estimated this gap at 4.3pp vs 4.8pp with overlapping CIs. Even with the more favorable numbers in the paper summary (2.1pp vs implied larger baselines), the comparison that matters most is against the simplest reasonable baseline: linear regression of scores on exposure.\n\nIf the full 4PL+gamma IRT machinery with NUTS inference (hours of computation) provides only ~1-2pp improvement over an OLS regression (seconds of computation), the practical case for ContamCal's complexity is weak. The paper should explicitly compute and report the paired difference with bootstrap CIs between ContamCal and linear regression.\n\n*Severity: Moderate. Does not invalidate the framework but raises cost-benefit questions.*\n\n**W8 (Minor). Prior specification for gamma differs between paper and Stan code.**\nThe paper states gamma ~ HalfNormal(0, 1) (constraining gamma >= 0, appropriate if contamination can only inflate scores). The Stan code uses gamma ~ std_normal() (unconstrained, allowing negative gamma). For the 0.5B case where contamination *decreases* performance, the unconstrained prior is actually more appropriate, but the inconsistency between paper and code should be resolved. The paper_framing.md also lists gamma ~ HalfNormal(0, 1), while the actual Stan model uses N(0, 1).\n\n*Severity: Minor but affects reproducibility.*\n\n**W9 (Minor). Prior on c and d differs between paper text and Stan code.**\nPaper Eq. 6: c ~ Beta(2, 8), d ~ Beta(8, 2). Stan code: c ~ Beta(5, 20), d ~ Beta(20, 5). The Beta(5, 20) prior centers c at 0.2 with tighter concentration than Beta(2, 8) which centers at 0.2 but with more variance. This is a minor inconsistency but should be reconciled for reproducibility.\n\n*Severity: Minor.*\n\n**W10 (Minor). Cross-family validation (Table 14, r=0.91) is encouraging but limited.**\nLlama-3.2-1B provides a single additional model family at a single scale. The r=0.91 correlation of gamma_k between Qwen and Llama is a positive signal, but with only 3 benchmarks contributing data points, the correlation is estimated from very few observations. A permutation test or bootstrap CI on this correlation would strengthen the claim.\n\n*Severity: Minor. The result is directionally positive.*\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. **ME model clarification**: Which model produced the results in Table 6—the measurement error model of Eq. 3 or the simple model that uses observed exposure directly? If the simple model, please revise the methods section to make this the primary specification and the ME model an ablation.\n\n2. **Gamma bias at N=32**: Your simulation analysis (exp-005) shows +23.6% positive gamma bias at N=50, sigma_e=0.1. What is the expected bias at N=32 (your actual pooled sample size)? Does this bias inflate or deflate the reported MAE?\n\n3. **S2/S4 at 1.5B/3B**: The paper claims S2 AUC >= 0.72 at >= 1.5B. Can you provide the actual empirical AUC values from your signal pipeline at these scales? The available 0.5B data shows S2 AUC = 0.466.\n\n4. **Ability regression at 0.5B**: With d100 MMLU dropping from 39.8% to 23.2%, ContamCal is effectively estimating ability *destruction* from SFT, not contamination *inflation*. How does gamma's interpretation change in this regime? Is a positive gamma still meaningful when the net effect is performance degradation?\n\n5. **Hierarchical gamma_k**: Have you fit a hierarchical model gamma_k ~ Normal(mu_gamma, sigma_gamma) to formally test benchmark heterogeneity? The 2.3pp MAE for hierarchical in Table 9 suggests you have—please clarify the specification.\n\n6. **Coverage diagnosis**: Can you provide posterior predictive checks showing the empirical item-level residual correlation structure? Is the 92% coverage stable across benchmarks, or is it driven by one benchmark's miscalibration?\n\n7. **Computational cost comparison**: What is the wall-clock time for ContamCal (signal computation + NUTS inference) versus linear regression, and does the MAE improvement justify the computational overhead?\n\n---\n\n## Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n1. **Simulation-based calibration (SBC) at actual N and N_items**: The paper reports SBC p=0.23, but details are sparse. SBC should be reported at the exact N_models and N_items used in the primary results, with rank uniformity plots.\n\n2. **Paired bootstrap test: ContamCal vs. linear regression**: A direct statistical test of whether the MAE difference is significant, not just point estimates.\n\n3. **Verbatim contamination condition**: All contamination is via paraphrases. A d100_verbatim condition (exact benchmark items in training) would validate whether the signals detect the most common real-world contamination mode.\n\n4. **Posterior predictive checks**: Item-level residual correlation analysis to diagnose local independence violations and their contribution to the coverage gap.\n\n5. **Sensitivity to RRF k parameter**: The k=60 default is standard but not validated for this application. A sweep over k in {10, 30, 60, 120} would assess robustness.\n\n6. **Full AUC x N simulation grid**: exp-004 has 48/120 conditions complete. The AUC=0.65 and AUC=0.90 conditions (0/60 seeds) would complete the identifiability phase diagram.\n\n---\n\n## Preliminary Score and Justification\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Justification**: The paper addresses an important and timely problem with a principled Bayesian approach grounded in educational measurement theory. The signal characterization findings (S1 scale-invariance, Min-K++ reversal) and Fisher Information analysis are genuine contributions with standalone value. However, several methodological concerns prevent acceptance:\n\n(1) The gap between the presented ME model and the actually-used simple model undermines the paper's distinctive UQ claim and raises reproducibility concerns.\n(2) The actual sample size (N~32) is in the bias-sensitive zone identified by the paper's own simulations, casting doubt on whether the headline gamma estimates are data-driven or prior-driven.\n(3) The multi-signal claim is not supported by verified empirical evidence at scales beyond 0.5B, where only S1 works.\n(4) The SFT injection protocol introduces multiple confounds (ability regression, format artifacts, position bias) that complicate interpretation of the calibration results.\n(5) The marginal advantage over linear regression raises questions about whether the full IRT machinery is justified.\n\n**Path to acceptance**: (a) Resolve the ME model discrepancy and be transparent about which model produced which results. (b) Either verify multi-signal effectiveness at larger scales or reframe honestly as single-signal + IRT. (c) Add more model families and seeds to push N above the identifiability threshold. (d) Provide definitive coverage gap diagnosis via SBC at actual experimental parameters. (e) Elevate the Min-K++ reversal and signal characterization as primary contributions—these may be the paper's strongest offerings.\n\n**Confidence: 4/5**\nI have examined the methodology, Stan model specifications, simulation results, and experimental data in detail. My main uncertainty is about whether the paper's reported results (Tables 6-14) reflect a later, more complete experimental run than the intermediate artifacts I can access, which would potentially resolve some of the concerns about N_models and multi-scale signal effectiveness. The methodological concerns about the ME model discrepancy, per-benchmark separate fits, and SFT confounds are independent of this uncertainty.","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4469,"cache_read_input_tokens":81486,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4469,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XBq1KVgdofAKMwiSTHMT2h","session_id":"9e2eb3c2-aea0-4799-9cc1-c104180c7ecf","uuid":"afcd1cf1-3197-45b9-b91f-270c65e1f2a6"}
